1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,925
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,925 --> 00:00:04,380
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:05,835
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:13,200
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK.

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:13,460
Yeah.

6
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:14,220
Welcome, everyone.

7
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:17,360
So now we're coming to another section of the course, which

8
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:18,340
is generative models.

9
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:21,000
We'll have three lectures on generative models.

10
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,080
And generative models are--

11
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:24,740
the new name for them is generative AI.

12
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:27,900
So this is the big thing of 2023,

13
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:32,119
and continuing on to this year, 2024.

14
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:32,619
OK.

15
00:00:32,619 --> 00:00:34,202
So we're going to learn all about what

16
00:00:34,202 --> 00:00:35,720
generative models are.

17
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:37,200
Again, this is one of those topics

18
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,660
which you could teach without mentioning deep learning at all.

19
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:41,785
Of course, we'll talk about deep generative models.

20
00:00:41,785 --> 00:00:45,760
But the place where generative models are having innovation

21
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:48,000
is mostly in deep learning.

22
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,280
They kind of go hand in hand.

23
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,280
OK.

24
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:54,200
So I want to come back to this picture

25
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,680
because we've had three lectures now on representation, learning,

26
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,370
and the way I like to think about generative modeling

27
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:02,630
is it's just the inverse of representation learning.

28
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:04,463
So these two things, representation learning

29
00:01:04,463 --> 00:01:07,570
and generative modeling, are very tightly coupled.

30
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:10,930
And we'll see, actually, in the next lecture, models

31
00:01:10,930 --> 00:01:13,530
that do both representation learning and generative

32
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:15,558
modeling at the same time.

33
00:01:15,558 --> 00:01:17,350
But we're talking about this direction now.

34
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:19,130
So we've so far, in the last few weeks,

35
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:21,170
talked a lot about going from data to some kind

36
00:01:21,170 --> 00:01:23,410
of low-dimensional embedding.

37
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:26,290
And now we'll be going from simple low-dimensional

38
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:28,510
embeddings to data.

39
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:29,010
OK.

40
00:01:29,010 --> 00:01:31,930
So today will be just a tour of a bunch

41
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:34,090
of the fundamentals of generative modeling

42
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:38,010
and some of the popular models like diffusion models and GANs.

43
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:40,210
And then, next week, we'll be looking

44
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:42,890
at variational autoencoders, which are a model that

45
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:46,050
does both directions jointly.

46
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:49,850
And then we'll talk about conditional models, which

47
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:52,850
is, How can I condition a generative model on some data

48
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:54,110
to predict some other data?

49
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:57,110
And in lecture 16, we'll really get out,

50
00:01:57,110 --> 00:01:58,870
What are these things useful for?

51
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:00,370
So it might be a little confusing when you first

52
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:01,620
come across generative models.

53
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:04,050
You know, like, why do we care about these things?

54
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:06,630
There are things that make random images, random stories.

55
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:07,770
Why would you care?

56
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:09,850
Well, we care because-- you know,

57
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:11,490
ChatGPT is a generative model, and we

58
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:13,530
know that that's something that's popular.

59
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:15,290
But we'll talk more about the applications

60
00:02:15,290 --> 00:02:18,210
of generative models in lecture 16.

61
00:02:18,210 --> 00:02:21,410
And yeah, I love generative models.

62
00:02:21,410 --> 00:02:23,750
I just think that--

63
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:25,950
my favorite topic is representation learning,

64
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:27,910
but my even more favorite topic is generative modeling.

65
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:29,660
And I think they're really the same topic.

66
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:31,830
And this is just, like, the coolest thing in AI.

67
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:35,850
So to me, generative models are kind

68
00:02:35,850 --> 00:02:37,590
of the core of intelligence.

69
00:02:37,590 --> 00:02:39,770
And that's kind of played out in the last few years

70
00:02:39,770 --> 00:02:42,170
with this generative AI revolution.

71
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:44,850
But it's just super cool stuff.

72
00:02:44,850 --> 00:02:46,170
OK.

73
00:02:46,170 --> 00:02:48,330
So I'm going to start by giving a little math

74
00:02:48,330 --> 00:02:50,890
background because we're going to be using

75
00:02:50,890 --> 00:02:53,130
a different type of math than we've used previously

76
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:55,310
in this class, which will be probability.

77
00:02:55,310 --> 00:02:57,560
This will be things that you should have already seen.

78
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,900
If you haven't, you should touch up on this.

79
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:00,400
OK.

80
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,620
Then we'll talk about, What's a generative model?

81
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:06,420
We'll talk about density functions, energy functions,

82
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:08,340
and samplers.

83
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:12,020
And then we'll get into a survey of a few of the currently

84
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:13,360
popular generative models.

85
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:15,838
So one will be the autoregressive model.

86
00:03:15,838 --> 00:03:17,380
The next will be the diffusion model.

87
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:19,820
And the next will be the GAN, the Generative Adversarial

88
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:20,700
Network.

89
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,373
So there's a lot.

90
00:03:22,373 --> 00:03:25,040
You can have a whole lecture, a whole class on diffusion models.

91
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,207
But we're just going to go through the basics of it.

92
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:29,340
And hopefully, you'll have this toolkit

93
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:32,640
that you can read up on in your own work.

94
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:33,140
OK.

95
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:36,740
And the good news is all generative models

96
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:37,880
use the same principles.

97
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,480
So it's not-- there's very tight connections between all of them.

98
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,500
You can often cast one model as another model

99
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,100
with some exchange of variables.

100
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,640
A diffusion model is a type of variational autoencoder,

101
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,420
and an autoregressive model is a small variation

102
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:52,360
on a diffusion model.

103
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:53,770
They're all connected.

104
00:03:53,770 --> 00:03:54,270
OK.

105
00:03:54,270 --> 00:03:57,145
But you don't know what those terms mean, presumably, yet.

106
00:03:57,145 --> 00:03:57,770
So don't worry.

107
00:03:57,770 --> 00:04:01,390
We're going to see some connections.

108
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:03,590
So what I want to start with is, yeah,

109
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:05,605
What is the definition of a generative model?

110
00:04:05,605 --> 00:04:07,730
And there's basically two definitions in the field.

111
00:04:07,730 --> 00:04:10,250
There's the definition that I will focus on,

112
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:13,110
which is an algorithm that generates data.

113
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:15,430
And there's a definition that you may have come across

114
00:04:15,430 --> 00:04:17,453
in a statistics class or a probability class,

115
00:04:17,453 --> 00:04:18,870
a slightly older definition, which

116
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:22,970
is the joint distribution of some data,

117
00:04:22,970 --> 00:04:26,750
so probability of a joint configuration of x's, y's,

118
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:29,590
and other variables

119
00:04:29,590 --> 00:04:30,090
OK.

120
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:33,670
So these are actually highly related concepts.

121
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:34,790
But I think it's--

122
00:04:34,790 --> 00:04:37,130
our real goal in generative modeling,

123
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:39,510
especially in this area that we would call generative AI,

124
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:42,010
is we want algorithms that make things that look like data.

125
00:04:42,010 --> 00:04:42,690
So what's data?

126
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:43,482
It could be images.

127
00:04:43,482 --> 00:04:45,230
We want algorithms that make images.

128
00:04:45,230 --> 00:04:46,170
What's data?

129
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:47,010
Proteins are data.

130
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:49,070
We want algorithms that make proteins,

131
00:04:49,070 --> 00:04:51,740
design proteins, design drugs.

132
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:54,060
We want algorithms that write stories and chat with us.

133
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:55,260
This is generating text.

134
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:56,320
That's data.

135
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:00,060
So this is in contrast to other forms of AI,

136
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:05,360
which are more about analyzing or classifying or deciding--

137
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:07,720
making decisions, taking data and saying,

138
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:10,320
this data is of type x, or this data

139
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:13,080
means I should turn left with my car.

140
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:16,773
So going from data to decisions or abstract representations

141
00:05:16,773 --> 00:05:18,440
is what we've seen before, but now we're

142
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:21,260
going from something to data.

143
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:21,760
OK.

144
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:25,160
But most of the time, we'll be generating data

145
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:26,960
by either explicitly or implicitly

146
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:28,380
modeling a distribution.

147
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:35,120
And I think that this is all probably

148
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:38,840
well known to you at this point in history,

149
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:42,280
but these things are all over the world now.

150
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,823
Generative models are what make it

151
00:05:43,823 --> 00:05:45,240
so that you can type in a sentence

152
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:49,360
on your favorite text-to-image model and say,

153
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:52,120
a photo of a group of robots building this data center,

154
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:54,660
and you get almost photorealistic pictures.

155
00:05:54,660 --> 00:05:56,800
Or you can make videos of this now.

156
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:00,640
This stuff is advancing so quickly.

157
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,280
But what you might not know as much,

158
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:04,360
or not see yet in the media as much,

159
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:06,360
is that generative models also can

160
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:11,480
be used to generate scientific data, data that is not just

161
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:14,440
for entertainment but might be for some kind of engineering

162
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,980
application or logistics data of some form.

163
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:20,140
Or in this case, this is a diffusion model,

164
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:21,560
a type of generative model, which

165
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:26,720
is generating some structures that describe

166
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:29,880
the geometry of a protein.

167
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,520
So we have this input, which is, like, I

168
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:35,320
want you to make me the--

169
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:38,900
I want you to tell me what the binding sites are on a protein,

170
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:42,320
and I'll use the diffusion model for that process.

171
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:43,380
Here's another example.

172
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:45,460
Here's an image-to-image model.

173
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:47,990
So it's generating-- the output of this model is an image.

174
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:50,450
But the output is a CT scan image,

175
00:06:50,450 --> 00:06:53,430
and this is conditioned on an input which is an MRI image.

176
00:06:53,430 --> 00:06:54,990
So it's taking it and it's saying,

177
00:06:54,990 --> 00:06:56,570
What would it look like if the doctor

178
00:06:56,570 --> 00:06:58,130
hadn't scanned with an MRI machine

179
00:06:58,130 --> 00:07:00,530
but had scanned with a CT machine?

180
00:07:00,530 --> 00:07:03,170
So we'll talk about that setting as well.

181
00:07:03,170 --> 00:07:05,930
OK, so generative models are not just for entertainment,

182
00:07:05,930 --> 00:07:09,250
but where they became popular was, yeah, writing funny stories

183
00:07:09,250 --> 00:07:12,530
and having chatbots and making funny images.

184
00:07:12,530 --> 00:07:14,570
OK.

185
00:07:14,570 --> 00:07:17,530
So let's go over a little bit of math background

186
00:07:17,530 --> 00:07:18,857
to get us all on the same page.

187
00:07:18,857 --> 00:07:21,190
And this is material that if you haven't seen it before,

188
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:21,870
you should review.

189
00:07:21,870 --> 00:07:23,870
But you should've taken some kind of probability

190
00:07:23,870 --> 00:07:25,250
or statistics class.

191
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:28,210
So we are going to be assuming that.

192
00:07:28,210 --> 00:07:28,902
OK.

193
00:07:28,902 --> 00:07:30,610
But we're going to be changing the way we

194
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:32,130
think about neural nets.

195
00:07:32,130 --> 00:07:35,050
So, so far, we've mostly thought of neural nets

196
00:07:35,050 --> 00:07:39,610
as functions that map from some input data to some output data.

197
00:07:39,610 --> 00:07:41,610
And in fact, we've thought of them

198
00:07:41,610 --> 00:07:43,970
as the classical definition of a function, which

199
00:07:43,970 --> 00:07:46,420
is, for each unique element of the--

200
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:48,380
for each element of the input space,

201
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:50,360
there is a unique element of the output space.

202
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:55,240
So it's a many-to-one mapping, or maybe a one-to-one mapping.

203
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:57,780
But it's not a one-to-many mapping.

204
00:07:57,780 --> 00:08:00,562
So for every element of the input, there's only one output.

205
00:08:00,562 --> 00:08:03,020
And that's not going to be sufficient for generative models

206
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:05,460
because we want to make we want to make

207
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:07,180
a whole distribution of things.

208
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:10,660
And so we're going to change our perspective a little bit.

209
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:13,420
Let me just give an example of the function perspective

210
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:14,940
on neural nets.

211
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:18,900
For an image classifier, the input might be a tensor,

212
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:22,783
an "N by M by C" number of color channels, a dimensional tensor.

213
00:08:22,783 --> 00:08:24,200
We're going to learn a function f.

214
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:26,420
It will be a neural network.

215
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:28,600
Our goal is to output a class prediction.

216
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,780
So it will be an integer from 1 to d, where

217
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:33,260
d is the number of classes

218
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:34,620
OK.

219
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,220
So instead, we're going to now think of neural nets

220
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:39,100
as-- or the models that we'll consider

221
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:42,260
are going to be mappings from some input space X,

222
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:46,980
not to a object Y, but to a distribution over objects Y.

223
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:49,820
So we call that, with this fancy script, P,

224
00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:54,420
the space of probability distributions over the space Y.

225
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:56,960
And that might seem a little bit weird,

226
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:59,420
but it's like going from data to a distribution

227
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:01,900
over possibilities, not just one answer.

228
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:03,583
And we've seen that, in fact, already.

229
00:09:03,583 --> 00:09:05,500
In fact, almost all the things that we've seen

230
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,125
or you'll encounter in machine learning

231
00:09:07,125 --> 00:09:08,980
can be recast from this perspective.

232
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:11,800
So one example is classification.

233
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:14,160
We actually model it as softmax regression.

234
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:17,920
This is a standard thing we've been doing in machine learning.

235
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:21,380
We learn a function from, in this case, let's say, images,

236
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:24,380
to a distribution over the probability

237
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:27,620
that the image contains each of the d classes.

238
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:30,420
So this little triangle is meant to indicate the simplex, which

239
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:34,900
is the space of all categorical distributions over d categories.

240
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:39,040
And for d categories, it's the d-minus-1 simplex.

241
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:42,570
So if I have a binary classifier, 0 or 1,

242
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:45,893
then my distribution will be, How much probability

243
00:09:45,893 --> 00:09:47,810
do I place on class 1 and how much probability

244
00:09:47,810 --> 00:09:52,470
do I place on class 2? f that will be a line.

245
00:09:52,470 --> 00:09:54,030
OK.

246
00:09:54,030 --> 00:09:59,090
So this is the way we'll think of what we're actually modeling.

247
00:09:59,090 --> 00:10:02,070
We're modeling the probability of some output conditioned

248
00:10:02,070 --> 00:10:04,670
on the input, which is thought of as a random variable,

249
00:10:04,670 --> 00:10:07,895
takes on some value x.

250
00:10:07,895 --> 00:10:09,270
And again, this is something that

251
00:10:09,270 --> 00:10:11,950
does characterize the classifiers we've

252
00:10:11,950 --> 00:10:13,510
seen in the past.

253
00:10:13,510 --> 00:10:17,630
If we have a vanilla regression, where we're doing regression

254
00:10:17,630 --> 00:10:20,790
with respect to a Euclidean like squared error loss,

255
00:10:20,790 --> 00:10:23,950
then you can recast that as outputting a Gaussian

256
00:10:23,950 --> 00:10:25,730
distribution over the possibilities,

257
00:10:25,730 --> 00:10:29,030
and the mean is what you're regressing.

258
00:10:29,030 --> 00:10:30,530
OK.

259
00:10:30,530 --> 00:10:33,510
So the main perspective here is that the outputs of neural nets

260
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:35,550
are, either implicitly or explicitly,

261
00:10:35,550 --> 00:10:37,770
going to be distributions of possibilities,

262
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:39,820
as opposed to just one, like, point prediction.

263
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:44,500
So this was in that hacker's guide lecture.

264
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:50,500
I talked about this idea that in a lot of deep learning,

265
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:54,360
we're going to be predicting something from something.

266
00:10:54,360 --> 00:10:55,800
And this little trick, this hack,

267
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,207
is that you make the thing you're

268
00:10:57,207 --> 00:10:59,040
conditioning on-- so you're going to predict

269
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:00,660
the class of an image.

270
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:03,960
But better than that would be to take your whole experience

271
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:06,880
of the photographer's life before they took that photo.

272
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:10,080
If you condition on more information, like a video,

273
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:12,560
then you get less uncertainty over the output.

274
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:14,320
And the neural nets we had looked

275
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:17,060
at before were making these point predictions, just saying,

276
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:18,602
for this input, I'm going to predict,

277
00:11:18,602 --> 00:11:21,320
What is the possible label Y, or what is the possible outcome Y?

278
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:24,025
But in reality, there's some set of possibilities.

279
00:11:24,025 --> 00:11:25,900
It's not always that there's only one answer.

280
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:28,000
There could be-- this image could

281
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
be a cat with probability of 50% and a dog

282
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,375
with another probability.

283
00:11:31,375 --> 00:11:33,500
And it becomes a complicated distribution to model.

284
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:36,280
And I said, just put more information into X

285
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:37,868
to reduce that uncertainty.

286
00:11:37,868 --> 00:11:39,410
But now I'm going to tell you, if you

287
00:11:39,410 --> 00:11:42,205
don't have that luxury, if you can't put more data into X,

288
00:11:42,205 --> 00:11:42,830
what do you do?

289
00:11:42,830 --> 00:11:45,550
How do you model a complicated output distribution

290
00:11:45,550 --> 00:11:47,690
that's not just a point?

291
00:11:47,690 --> 00:11:50,250
OK.

292
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:50,750
OK.

293
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:53,630
So that's what generative modeling tries to deal with.

294
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:56,170
So a little bit more notation--

295
00:11:56,170 --> 00:11:57,950
so we'll talk about random variables.

296
00:11:57,950 --> 00:12:00,150
They'll be characterized by a distribution.

297
00:12:00,150 --> 00:12:07,670
So a random variable is a set of possible atoms,

298
00:12:07,670 --> 00:12:10,970
and there's a distribution over what

299
00:12:10,970 --> 00:12:13,070
values that random variable will take on.

300
00:12:13,070 --> 00:12:16,030
And that distribution is either discrete or continuous.

301
00:12:16,030 --> 00:12:18,790
If it's discrete, we call it a probability mass function.

302
00:12:18,790 --> 00:12:21,490
And if it's continuous, we will call it a probability density

303
00:12:21,490 --> 00:12:24,210
function.

304
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:26,850
And a realization of that random variable

305
00:12:26,850 --> 00:12:29,770
will be indicated with a lowercase variable name.

306
00:12:29,770 --> 00:12:32,130
So a sample from the distribution

307
00:12:32,130 --> 00:12:34,890
of possible settings of that random variable

308
00:12:34,890 --> 00:12:36,330
will be a realization x.

309
00:12:36,330 --> 00:12:39,330
And we'll call that the probability of x.

310
00:12:39,330 --> 00:12:41,790
And you can think of that as a mass, in the discrete case,

311
00:12:41,790 --> 00:12:45,550
or a probability density of x, in the continuous case.

312
00:12:45,550 --> 00:12:46,050
OK.

313
00:12:46,050 --> 00:12:49,525
So uppercase letters, in this course, are random variables.

314
00:12:49,525 --> 00:12:51,150
They're characterized by distributions.

315
00:12:51,150 --> 00:12:52,950
Lowercase letters are realizations.

316
00:12:52,950 --> 00:12:55,930
They're characterized by scalars, just the probability

317
00:12:55,930 --> 00:12:59,270
of that realization.

318
00:12:59,270 --> 00:12:59,770
OK.

319
00:12:59,770 --> 00:13:03,570
So just, formally, a probability mass function

320
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:06,330
is a mapping from data to scalars.

321
00:13:06,330 --> 00:13:08,990
And the scalars are between 0 and 1.

322
00:13:08,990 --> 00:13:12,210
So the probability can be 0 up to a hundred percent, up to 1.

323
00:13:12,210 --> 00:13:15,650
And the sum of all the probabilities

324
00:13:15,650 --> 00:13:17,910
over all possibilities sums to 1.

325
00:13:17,910 --> 00:13:20,050
That's just the definition of probability.

326
00:13:20,050 --> 00:13:22,175
So if you ever have a vector that sums to 1-- like,

327
00:13:22,175 --> 00:13:24,170
let's say you do L1 normalization

328
00:13:24,170 --> 00:13:27,410
of a vector of activations in a neural network, and all of the--

329
00:13:27,410 --> 00:13:31,290
and also, you have a sigmoid, so that the numbers are

330
00:13:31,290 --> 00:13:33,170
all between 0 and 1-- well, that vector, you

331
00:13:33,170 --> 00:13:35,360
can call that a probability mass function.

332
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:39,857
That's just mathematically what a probability mass function is.

333
00:13:39,857 --> 00:13:41,440
Probability density is the same thing,

334
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:44,700
except it's over a continuous space and it integrates to 1.

335
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:48,600
And the densities don't have to be-- they can go to infinity.

336
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:51,460
So it's just that the densities are nonnegative.

337
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:52,540
OK.

338
00:13:52,540 --> 00:13:54,780
So again, this should be stuff from probability,

339
00:13:54,780 --> 00:13:55,653
intro probability.

340
00:13:55,653 --> 00:13:57,820
But it's a little bit of a different way of thinking

341
00:13:57,820 --> 00:13:59,860
about things, and it's the fundamental language

342
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:02,300
of generative modeling.

343
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,160
So you should refresh yourself on that.

344
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:06,820
And the notation that is linked in the schedule for the very

345
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:09,960
first class, and that you should be using on your problem sets

346
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:11,860
and we're using throughout the class,

347
00:14:11,860 --> 00:14:13,140
has a section on probability.

348
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:14,820
And we have some shorthands.

349
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:18,020
So one shorthand that might confuse you a little

350
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:21,840
is, if I write p of capital letter X,

351
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:24,760
I mean a distribution of this random variable.

352
00:14:24,760 --> 00:14:27,140
And if I write p of lowercase letter x,

353
00:14:27,140 --> 00:14:30,300
I mean the scalar probability of random variable X

354
00:14:30,300 --> 00:14:31,790
taking on value x.

355
00:14:31,790 --> 00:14:37,070
So this is shorthand for the distribution over X,

356
00:14:37,070 --> 00:14:40,270
and this is shorthand for the distribution--

357
00:14:40,270 --> 00:14:43,510
sorry, the scalar probability of the realization random variable

358
00:14:43,510 --> 00:14:45,830
X takes on the value little x.

359
00:14:45,830 --> 00:14:49,730
So this is on this slide as well, from the notation doc.

360
00:14:53,110 --> 00:14:53,610
OK.

361
00:14:53,610 --> 00:14:54,110
Cool.

362
00:14:54,110 --> 00:14:58,150
Any questions about the math background before we

363
00:14:58,150 --> 00:15:02,590
now get into generative models?

364
00:15:02,590 --> 00:15:04,150
Yeah?

365
00:15:04,150 --> 00:15:07,750
AUDIENCE: When you say continuous probability function,

366
00:15:07,750 --> 00:15:10,430
how can you define the density of X?

367
00:15:10,430 --> 00:15:13,910
I mean, my understanding is that it's always zero.

368
00:15:13,910 --> 00:15:15,270
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

369
00:15:15,270 --> 00:15:15,930
Great question.

370
00:15:15,930 --> 00:15:19,160
So the question is, For probability density functions,

371
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:20,410
how do you define probability?

372
00:15:20,410 --> 00:15:23,550
The probability of an infinitesimal thing

373
00:15:23,550 --> 00:15:24,530
shouldn't go to 0.

374
00:15:24,530 --> 00:15:27,990
So the definition is you always only define

375
00:15:27,990 --> 00:15:29,590
the probability of a variable taking

376
00:15:29,590 --> 00:15:32,390
on some value over an interval, so the probability

377
00:15:32,390 --> 00:15:36,133
of x being between 0 and 0.1.

378
00:15:36,133 --> 00:15:38,050
So it's all defined with respect to integrals.

379
00:15:38,050 --> 00:15:40,047
But usually, we don't worry about that.

380
00:15:40,047 --> 00:15:41,630
This is, like, when you start thinking

381
00:15:41,630 --> 00:15:44,005
about measure theory and the proper way to define things.

382
00:15:44,005 --> 00:15:45,713
But just think of it like the probability

383
00:15:45,713 --> 00:15:46,890
that X takes on that value.

384
00:15:46,890 --> 00:15:48,830
It's really defined as some integral.

385
00:15:48,830 --> 00:15:49,510
Yeah?

386
00:15:49,510 --> 00:15:50,910
AUDIENCE: Could you explain one more time

387
00:15:50,910 --> 00:15:52,510
how we can think of a neural network

388
00:15:52,510 --> 00:15:54,237
as an implicit distribution?

389
00:15:54,237 --> 00:15:55,070
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

390
00:15:55,070 --> 00:15:56,570
How can we think of a neural network

391
00:15:56,570 --> 00:15:58,250
as implying a probability distribution?

392
00:15:58,250 --> 00:16:01,270
Well, I'll show a bunch of examples of this.

393
00:16:01,270 --> 00:16:03,910
But basically, let's say that I'm doing regression

394
00:16:03,910 --> 00:16:05,250
with an L2 loss.

395
00:16:05,250 --> 00:16:07,190
I'm going to predict some number.

396
00:16:07,190 --> 00:16:10,270
I will interpret that as, I am predicting

397
00:16:10,270 --> 00:16:13,710
a distribution over outcomes, parameterized by the mean

398
00:16:13,710 --> 00:16:16,630
equals my output of my neural network.

399
00:16:16,630 --> 00:16:21,910
So that's like-- and that interpretation is valid

400
00:16:21,910 --> 00:16:26,430
because the probability of the data under that Gaussian model

401
00:16:26,430 --> 00:16:29,520
is equal to the squared error loss function.

402
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:32,320
So this is something-- you can look

403
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:35,640
into the connection between Gaussian max likelihood modeling

404
00:16:35,640 --> 00:16:38,580
and least squares regression.

405
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:39,360
AUDIENCE: OK.

406
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:44,340
So it's not that the network is sampling of a distribution,

407
00:16:44,340 --> 00:16:46,340
it's that the output is a distribution itself?

408
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:47,220
PHILLIP ISOLA: We'll see both.

409
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:48,700
So there's two possibilities.

410
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:53,080
One, the network is outputting samples from a distribution,

411
00:16:53,080 --> 00:16:55,040
and the other is the network is outputting

412
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:56,300
parameters of a distribution.

413
00:16:56,300 --> 00:16:59,190
So I'm going to talk about both.

414
00:16:59,190 --> 00:16:59,940
OK.

415
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,600
So let's build up some intuition.

416
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:04,060
So that was math background.

417
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:05,477
We're going to get some intuition,

418
00:17:05,477 --> 00:17:07,920
and then we're going to get back to the math.

419
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:10,839
So I like to start with generative models

420
00:17:10,839 --> 00:17:13,560
from this perspective, that we already

421
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:15,040
know about things like classifiers

422
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:16,677
take data and output a label.

423
00:17:16,677 --> 00:17:18,760
And so again, I like thinking of generative models

424
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:21,440
as the inverse of that, the opposite direction.

425
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:24,319
Take a label or some abstract description, like,

426
00:17:24,319 --> 00:17:26,530
I want you to make me a bird, and output the thing

427
00:17:26,530 --> 00:17:28,410
that we would normally call data.

428
00:17:28,410 --> 00:17:30,570
There's no definition to label versus data.

429
00:17:30,570 --> 00:17:33,150
But you kind of have an intuition about what that means.

430
00:17:33,150 --> 00:17:35,130
So, yeah, precisely, mathematically, there's

431
00:17:35,130 --> 00:17:36,790
really no distinction here.

432
00:17:36,790 --> 00:17:40,770
But generative models are when the outputs are something

433
00:17:40,770 --> 00:17:42,970
you would think of as data.

434
00:17:42,970 --> 00:17:44,946
OK.

435
00:17:44,946 --> 00:17:48,490
So what is the problem?

436
00:17:48,490 --> 00:17:50,650
The problem is that in this direction,

437
00:17:50,650 --> 00:17:53,650
there's many possible outputs that match the input.

438
00:17:53,650 --> 00:17:57,010
So in this direction, there's only one possible label

439
00:17:57,010 --> 00:17:59,170
that is the correct label for this bird.

440
00:17:59,170 --> 00:18:02,530
But in this direction, that robin

441
00:18:02,530 --> 00:18:04,430
is just one possibility out of many.

442
00:18:04,430 --> 00:18:07,610
So there's the distribution over correct answers.

443
00:18:07,610 --> 00:18:10,210
So how do we model the distribution?

444
00:18:10,210 --> 00:18:12,150
So we're going to see a few different ways.

445
00:18:12,150 --> 00:18:15,290
But here's one of the ways.

446
00:18:15,290 --> 00:18:18,990
We take a function generator, called a generator.

447
00:18:18,990 --> 00:18:20,490
That's a neural net.

448
00:18:20,490 --> 00:18:23,010
It's a deterministic function, but we condition it

449
00:18:23,010 --> 00:18:25,020
on some random variable.

450
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:28,820
So we make multiple outputs by taking the function

451
00:18:28,820 --> 00:18:31,553
and conditioning it on some random variable.

452
00:18:31,553 --> 00:18:32,720
And that will be these dice.

453
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:33,760
So we roll the dice.

454
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:35,010
We input that into our system.

455
00:18:35,010 --> 00:18:35,940
We get one outcome.

456
00:18:35,940 --> 00:18:36,648
We roll the dice.

457
00:18:36,648 --> 00:18:38,232
That's a different setting of numbers,

458
00:18:38,232 --> 00:18:39,700
and that gives us another outcome.

459
00:18:39,700 --> 00:18:43,060
And this is the way that you can turn a normal neural network

460
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:46,460
into something that can sample from a distribution,

461
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:50,340
by conditioning it on random variables.

462
00:18:50,340 --> 00:18:53,140
OK.

463
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:55,860
So sometimes, people will call these random variables--

464
00:18:55,860 --> 00:18:57,620
these dice, in this diagram--

465
00:18:57,620 --> 00:18:59,340
as noise.

466
00:18:59,340 --> 00:19:02,120
I think that's a confusing term.

467
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:04,970
They're noise in the sense that they are random variables,

468
00:19:04,970 --> 00:19:06,660
so they're randomized.

469
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:09,140
But they're not noise in the sense that they're meaningless

470
00:19:09,140 --> 00:19:11,380
or something that you want to get rid of or ignore.

471
00:19:11,380 --> 00:19:14,780
These dice rolls, you should instead think of them

472
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:19,140
as specifying everything about the data that's not specified

473
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:20,280
by your input command.

474
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:21,870
So if I say, I want to make a bird,

475
00:19:21,870 --> 00:19:23,620
well, everything else that's not specified

476
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:26,328
by that-- like what color is the bird, what species is the bird--

477
00:19:26,328 --> 00:19:29,140
has to be specified, made deterministic by,

478
00:19:29,140 --> 00:19:31,220
determined by these random variables.

479
00:19:31,220 --> 00:19:34,920
So they're specifying, What's the color of the bird?

480
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:37,392
If I roll that, I'll change the color of the bird.

481
00:19:37,392 --> 00:19:38,600
What's the angle of the bird?

482
00:19:38,600 --> 00:19:40,787
If I roll that, I'll change the angle of the bird.

483
00:19:40,787 --> 00:19:42,120
And what's the size of the bird?

484
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:43,720
OK.

485
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:47,020
So that's kind of how a generative model will

486
00:19:47,020 --> 00:19:50,720
output randomized possible birds for the input command,

487
00:19:50,720 --> 00:19:53,140
make me a photo of a bird.

488
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:54,540
OK.

489
00:19:54,540 --> 00:19:58,057
So rather than thinking of them as dice rolls, another way,

490
00:19:58,057 --> 00:20:00,140
you could think of them as these sort of control--

491
00:20:00,140 --> 00:20:03,620
the underlying control variables that control the output

492
00:20:03,620 --> 00:20:05,940
that you're going to get.

493
00:20:05,940 --> 00:20:08,060
And I could spin this knob randomly,

494
00:20:08,060 --> 00:20:10,100
and then it's like noise, a random variable,

495
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:11,480
that gives me a random bird.

496
00:20:11,480 --> 00:20:14,700
But I could also set that knob, that dice-- now

497
00:20:14,700 --> 00:20:17,460
I'm going to think of it as a knob-- to a value of the bird

498
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:18,380
I want.

499
00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:22,098
And this is something that we'll see in a lot of the applications

500
00:20:22,098 --> 00:20:24,390
of generative models, gearing the models by controlling

501
00:20:24,390 --> 00:20:26,990
the latent variables that are input into the models.

502
00:20:26,990 --> 00:20:28,270
OK.

503
00:20:28,270 --> 00:20:28,810
OK.

504
00:20:28,810 --> 00:20:31,393
So this is all kind of to build up this intuition about what's

505
00:20:31,393 --> 00:20:33,230
going on.

506
00:20:33,230 --> 00:20:36,270
Now let's-- I want to have you make a generative model really

507
00:20:36,270 --> 00:20:36,770
quickly.

508
00:20:36,770 --> 00:20:40,790
So one of the places where generative modeling got

509
00:20:40,790 --> 00:20:43,070
started is statistics, but another place where it got

510
00:20:43,070 --> 00:20:45,297
started is computer graphics, actually.

511
00:20:45,297 --> 00:20:47,630
So in computer graphics, the name for a generative model

512
00:20:47,630 --> 00:20:51,390
is-- the classical name is procedural graphics.

513
00:20:51,390 --> 00:20:54,910
It's a way of making random content for your video game

514
00:20:54,910 --> 00:20:55,730
or movie.

515
00:20:55,730 --> 00:20:58,530
I want to draw a random map for my video game,

516
00:20:58,530 --> 00:21:01,770
so I will write down a procedure that creates a random map.

517
00:21:01,770 --> 00:21:02,950
That's a generative model.

518
00:21:02,950 --> 00:21:05,710
So let's make a generative model like that.

519
00:21:05,710 --> 00:21:08,810
So we're going to take in a random variable.

520
00:21:08,810 --> 00:21:11,870
It will be a coin flip, so a Bernoulli random variable.

521
00:21:11,870 --> 00:21:15,530
And we're going to try to make a generative model of rivers.

522
00:21:15,530 --> 00:21:17,670
So here's how it's going to be.

523
00:21:17,670 --> 00:21:20,480
Does anyone have a coin?

524
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:22,880
Somebody has a coin?

525
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:25,677
An object you can flip.

526
00:21:25,677 --> 00:21:27,260
Google can do it for you, if you want.

527
00:21:27,260 --> 00:21:30,080
You can search "coin flip."

528
00:21:30,080 --> 00:21:31,080
AUDIENCE: I have a coin.

529
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:31,620
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK, good.

530
00:21:31,620 --> 00:21:32,662
So why don't you do this?

531
00:21:32,662 --> 00:21:35,505
So here's the start of the river.

532
00:21:35,505 --> 00:21:37,880
So flip the coin, and it will determine if the river goes

533
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:39,200
left or right.

534
00:21:39,200 --> 00:21:40,200
AUDIENCE: Heads is left?

535
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:40,760
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK.

536
00:21:40,760 --> 00:21:41,260
Yeah.

537
00:21:41,260 --> 00:21:42,173
Heads is left.

538
00:21:42,173 --> 00:21:42,840
AUDIENCE: Heads.

539
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:43,200
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK.

540
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:43,700
Left.

541
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:45,968
Again.

542
00:21:45,968 --> 00:21:46,800
AUDIENCE: Heads.

543
00:21:46,800 --> 00:21:47,080
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK.

544
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:47,600
Left.

545
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:50,025
Again.

546
00:21:50,025 --> 00:21:50,692
AUDIENCE: Heads.

547
00:21:50,692 --> 00:21:51,680
[LAUGHTER]

548
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:52,980
PHILLIP ISOLA: So I don't know if you can still see it,

549
00:21:52,980 --> 00:21:54,147
but anyways, going that way.

550
00:21:54,147 --> 00:21:54,893
One more time?

551
00:21:54,893 --> 00:21:55,560
AUDIENCE: Tails.

552
00:21:55,560 --> 00:21:56,440
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK, cool.

553
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:58,023
So I think that's a pretty cool river.

554
00:21:58,023 --> 00:21:59,840
And then my computer rendering engine

555
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:03,720
would probably make it a little thicker, smooth out the borders,

556
00:22:03,720 --> 00:22:04,720
paint it blue.

557
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:06,060
And then there I go.

558
00:22:06,060 --> 00:22:09,600
I have randomly generated a river from a distribution.

559
00:22:09,600 --> 00:22:11,380
You could do the same trick with trees.

560
00:22:11,380 --> 00:22:12,860
You could flip two coins.

561
00:22:12,860 --> 00:22:15,062
One coin is, like, how many branches-- maybe

562
00:22:15,062 --> 00:22:17,520
roll a dice, how many branches, and then the other coin is,

563
00:22:17,520 --> 00:22:19,120
like, What's the angle of the branch?

564
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:20,870
So you can write little scripts like this.

565
00:22:20,870 --> 00:22:24,840
You probably have done it in some of your coding experience.

566
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:25,600
OK.

567
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:28,400
So I wrote a little program that does that.

568
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:30,452
It's just exactly what we did.

569
00:22:30,452 --> 00:22:31,660
So what is the program doing?

570
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:37,640
It's flipping a coin N times and drawing the river by just

571
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:41,100
changing the heading of the river by some 10-degree angle.

572
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:43,540
Depending on if I flip heads or tails,

573
00:22:43,540 --> 00:22:45,680
it will choose left or right.

574
00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:46,400
OK.

575
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:47,460
So what is that?

576
00:22:47,460 --> 00:22:51,620
That's a mapping from random variables to generated imagery.

577
00:22:51,620 --> 00:22:55,720
And that's how generative models tend to work.

578
00:22:55,720 --> 00:22:59,520
So here's a more complicated mapping.

579
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:03,480
But again, you can specify so many details of the world

580
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:07,488
just via choosing a random variable that determines each

581
00:23:07,488 --> 00:23:08,780
of the attributes of the image.

582
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:11,660
So in this case, I have a Bernoulli variable, N of them,

583
00:23:11,660 --> 00:23:12,880
for the river turns.

584
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:15,830
I have a Gaussian variable for the color of the grass.

585
00:23:15,830 --> 00:23:17,427
It's brighter or darker.

586
00:23:17,427 --> 00:23:19,510
I have a uniform variable for the number of trees.

587
00:23:19,510 --> 00:23:22,850
This is a top-down look at some river.

588
00:23:22,850 --> 00:23:25,830
And you could say those random variables are noise.

589
00:23:25,830 --> 00:23:29,210
But what we'll think of them as is

590
00:23:29,210 --> 00:23:32,730
as control knobs that specify all the attributes of the data.

591
00:23:32,730 --> 00:23:35,550
And a formal name for that is latent variables.

592
00:23:35,550 --> 00:23:38,930
So a latent variable is any variable

593
00:23:38,930 --> 00:23:41,570
that determines how the data looks

594
00:23:41,570 --> 00:23:44,210
that is not directly observed.

595
00:23:44,210 --> 00:23:45,430
OK.

596
00:23:45,430 --> 00:23:47,930
So these latent variables are the thing

597
00:23:47,930 --> 00:23:50,710
that underlie the causal process of generating the data.

598
00:23:53,730 --> 00:23:55,230
OK.

599
00:23:55,230 --> 00:23:57,530
So yeah, that's one concept to start with,

600
00:23:57,530 --> 00:24:00,770
is that generative models that we'll see

601
00:24:00,770 --> 00:24:04,240
are going to be taking noise as input and turning it into data.

602
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:06,490
And you should really think of the noise as the latent

603
00:24:06,490 --> 00:24:07,230
variables.

604
00:24:10,210 --> 00:24:14,880
OK So now let's talk about how to learn such models.

605
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:17,980
So you could handcraft this, write down a Python script

606
00:24:17,980 --> 00:24:19,580
or play a game in a class.

607
00:24:19,580 --> 00:24:22,420
But how do you actually learn that program

608
00:24:22,420 --> 00:24:25,632
and represent it as a neural net?

609
00:24:25,632 --> 00:24:27,840
And this gets to the question that was asked earlier.

610
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:30,300
There's going to be two different approaches.

611
00:24:30,300 --> 00:24:32,540
One, I'll call the direct approach.

612
00:24:32,540 --> 00:24:34,300
The direct approach is I'm going to learn

613
00:24:34,300 --> 00:24:40,760
a function that directly samples random generated data points.

614
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:41,260
OK.

615
00:24:41,260 --> 00:24:43,540
It'll start from z, which is going

616
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:46,720
to be our name for the noise, or the latent variables,

617
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:48,660
so the dice rolls or the coin flips.

618
00:24:48,660 --> 00:24:50,980
And it will output data.

619
00:24:50,980 --> 00:24:53,460
And the other approach will be, we'll call,

620
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:55,940
the indirect approach, which is, rather than learning

621
00:24:55,940 --> 00:24:59,900
a function that goes from random variables to data,

622
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:03,740
I'll learn a function that scores the probability of a data

623
00:25:03,740 --> 00:25:04,900
point.

624
00:25:04,900 --> 00:25:06,980
So this is a function that should assign

625
00:25:06,980 --> 00:25:11,950
a high score, a high scalar, to data that I like

626
00:25:11,950 --> 00:25:14,470
and a low number to data that I don't like.

627
00:25:14,470 --> 00:25:17,110
And there's a lot of different varieties of what that scoring

628
00:25:17,110 --> 00:25:18,668
function could be.

629
00:25:18,668 --> 00:25:21,210
The most basic one would be, it's the likelihood of the data,

630
00:25:21,210 --> 00:25:23,510
the probability of the data, is a mapping

631
00:25:23,510 --> 00:25:25,710
from the data to a scalar.

632
00:25:25,710 --> 00:25:29,990
If it's a density, it's a nonnegative scalar.

633
00:25:29,990 --> 00:25:30,910
OK.

634
00:25:30,910 --> 00:25:32,850
Then, if I have such a scoring function,

635
00:25:32,850 --> 00:25:36,190
I can generate data by searching for data points that

636
00:25:36,190 --> 00:25:40,110
score highly, or by sampling from the distribution

637
00:25:40,110 --> 00:25:43,030
given by that score.

638
00:25:43,030 --> 00:25:47,693
So it's like the direct approach directly give me data points.

639
00:25:47,693 --> 00:25:49,110
Indirect approach gives me a score

640
00:25:49,110 --> 00:25:51,590
that I can optimize to find good data points,

641
00:25:51,590 --> 00:25:55,710
or data points that are high probability.

642
00:25:55,710 --> 00:25:58,657
Notation wise, there is kind of a historical name

643
00:25:58,657 --> 00:26:00,990
for this top one, which is called an implicit generative

644
00:26:00,990 --> 00:26:01,290
model.

645
00:26:01,290 --> 00:26:03,690
I'm not sure that that name is still around or sticking around,

646
00:26:03,690 --> 00:26:04,898
but you might come across it.

647
00:26:04,898 --> 00:26:06,670
And if you do, don't worry.

648
00:26:06,670 --> 00:26:09,090
Implicit sounds like it's kind of indirect,

649
00:26:09,090 --> 00:26:10,670
but this is the direct approach.

650
00:26:10,670 --> 00:26:13,110
So what it means is that the sampling procedure

651
00:26:13,110 --> 00:26:14,950
implies a probability distribution

652
00:26:14,950 --> 00:26:16,390
you're sampling from.

653
00:26:16,390 --> 00:26:18,430
That's why it's called implicit.

654
00:26:18,430 --> 00:26:20,945
So this direct sampler implies a distribution

655
00:26:20,945 --> 00:26:22,570
that you could have drawn samples from.

656
00:26:22,570 --> 00:26:25,510
And this distribution, this scoring function,

657
00:26:25,510 --> 00:26:30,190
implies a sampler that can generate data directly.

658
00:26:30,190 --> 00:26:34,610
So we'll see both of these types of models in more detail.

659
00:26:34,610 --> 00:26:35,110
OK.

660
00:26:35,110 --> 00:26:36,972
So let's start with the direct approach.

661
00:26:36,972 --> 00:26:38,430
The direct approach is the one that

662
00:26:38,430 --> 00:26:42,350
is kind of the more popular characterization right

663
00:26:42,350 --> 00:26:43,990
now, whereas the indirect approach is

664
00:26:43,990 --> 00:26:45,470
the more classical characterization

665
00:26:45,470 --> 00:26:47,630
of generative models.

666
00:26:47,630 --> 00:26:49,648
So the direct approach is, I start with--

667
00:26:49,648 --> 00:26:51,690
I train the model by giving it a lot of examples.

668
00:26:51,690 --> 00:26:52,850
It's just regular machine learning.

669
00:26:52,850 --> 00:26:54,310
Right, I give you examples, and I

670
00:26:54,310 --> 00:26:57,070
try to make more stuff like the examples.

671
00:26:57,070 --> 00:26:58,890
And I'll take the data.

672
00:26:58,890 --> 00:27:01,670
I'll output parameters of a parameterized function that

673
00:27:01,670 --> 00:27:04,750
can map from random noise or from latent variables that

674
00:27:04,750 --> 00:27:06,440
are randomized to--

675
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:09,280
that have simple distributions, to data samples that

676
00:27:09,280 --> 00:27:11,040
look like the training data.

677
00:27:11,040 --> 00:27:14,040
So the objective is to make more data that

678
00:27:14,040 --> 00:27:16,240
looks like the training data.

679
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:16,740
OK.

680
00:27:16,740 --> 00:27:19,548
So this, in our class, will be a neural network.

681
00:27:19,548 --> 00:27:21,340
But it doesn't have to be a neural network.

682
00:27:21,340 --> 00:27:23,880
But that's the thing that tends to work the best.

683
00:27:23,880 --> 00:27:26,780
And we call these two phases training.

684
00:27:26,780 --> 00:27:28,200
And now we use a different term.

685
00:27:28,200 --> 00:27:29,900
Rather than testing, we might call it sampling.

686
00:27:29,900 --> 00:27:31,483
But it's the same as the testing phase

687
00:27:31,483 --> 00:27:33,493
for any neural net that's fit to data.

688
00:27:33,493 --> 00:27:35,160
It's just, when I run it, you might call

689
00:27:35,160 --> 00:27:37,117
it inference or deployment.

690
00:27:37,117 --> 00:27:39,200
But from a generative model, we can also just call

691
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:41,480
it sampling from the model.

692
00:27:41,480 --> 00:27:44,285
OK.

693
00:27:44,285 --> 00:27:48,140
So the indirect approach is--

694
00:27:48,140 --> 00:27:50,640
I already described it, but let's look at it in a little bit

695
00:27:50,640 --> 00:27:51,880
more detail here.

696
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:54,680
So it is, take some data-- and this

697
00:27:54,680 --> 00:27:57,240
is what you would've seen in your statistics

698
00:27:57,240 --> 00:27:59,120
classes or probability classes.

699
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:03,440
You take some data and you learn a scoring function

700
00:28:03,440 --> 00:28:06,890
that places a high score where the data lives.

701
00:28:06,890 --> 00:28:07,490
OK.

702
00:28:07,490 --> 00:28:11,290
So the most standard one would be a probability distribution

703
00:28:11,290 --> 00:28:14,050
that has high probability where the data lives,

704
00:28:14,050 --> 00:28:16,210
so that the samples that you observe

705
00:28:16,210 --> 00:28:21,050
are samples from the distribution that you learn.

706
00:28:21,050 --> 00:28:23,650
But it could also be what we'll call an energy function, which

707
00:28:23,650 --> 00:28:25,317
I'll show you, or something more exotic,

708
00:28:25,317 --> 00:28:27,650
some other type of score function.

709
00:28:27,650 --> 00:28:29,550
And then, sampling is no longer direct.

710
00:28:29,550 --> 00:28:32,130
Now, sampling is, I have my function that

711
00:28:32,130 --> 00:28:36,030
scores the probability of every possible value of x.

712
00:28:36,030 --> 00:28:38,550
So this is a one-dimensional distribution, the x-axis.

713
00:28:38,550 --> 00:28:41,170
What's the probability of coming up with this value of x?

714
00:28:41,170 --> 00:28:42,690
It's high probability.

715
00:28:42,690 --> 00:28:45,370
And I can sample from that distribution using

716
00:28:45,370 --> 00:28:48,770
another algorithm, which will be called a sampling algorithm.

717
00:28:48,770 --> 00:28:53,230
And we won't go into too much detail about these.

718
00:28:53,230 --> 00:28:57,890
But you probably-- you may have seen these before as--

719
00:28:57,890 --> 00:29:00,600
Markov chain Monte Carlo is one type of sampling algorithm.

720
00:29:00,600 --> 00:29:02,350
Another type is called rejection sampling.

721
00:29:02,350 --> 00:29:05,450
You just take a random sample and reject it in proportion

722
00:29:05,450 --> 00:29:07,745
to its probability under that scoring function.

723
00:29:07,745 --> 00:29:09,370
So there's a lot of sampling algorithms

724
00:29:09,370 --> 00:29:11,010
for how to go from this distribution

725
00:29:11,010 --> 00:29:14,190
to samples from that distribution.

726
00:29:14,190 --> 00:29:14,690
OK.

727
00:29:14,690 --> 00:29:16,550
So this is the indirect approach.

728
00:29:20,980 --> 00:29:24,610
So what's the goal of generative modeling?

729
00:29:24,610 --> 00:29:29,105
The goal is to make synthetic data that looks like real data,

730
00:29:29,105 --> 00:29:31,230
make more stuff that looks like your training data.

731
00:29:34,830 --> 00:29:36,788
So the modeling questions to answer are,

732
00:29:36,788 --> 00:29:38,830
What does it mean to look like the training data?

733
00:29:38,830 --> 00:29:40,810
What does it mean for it to be making more stuff that's

734
00:29:40,810 --> 00:29:41,710
like the training data?

735
00:29:41,710 --> 00:29:43,002
It doesn't have to be an image.

736
00:29:43,002 --> 00:29:46,637
It could be "looks like" in an abstract sense.

737
00:29:46,637 --> 00:29:48,970
And there's a lot of answers to this question, that lead

738
00:29:48,970 --> 00:29:50,970
to different types of model.

739
00:29:50,970 --> 00:29:53,050
But we've kind of converged on one main one

740
00:29:53,050 --> 00:29:56,610
in machine learning, which is that you should make

741
00:29:56,610 --> 00:30:00,860
more data that has high probability under a density

742
00:30:00,860 --> 00:30:04,060
model fit to your training data.

743
00:30:04,060 --> 00:30:06,460
So this is called the max likelihood objective.

744
00:30:06,460 --> 00:30:09,085
We're going to go through that in a little bit more detail now.

745
00:30:11,140 --> 00:30:13,160
So here's, again, the density model.

746
00:30:13,160 --> 00:30:16,340
It's a mapping from data to positive numbers--

747
00:30:16,340 --> 00:30:18,500
or nonnegative numbers, in fact.

748
00:30:18,500 --> 00:30:20,390
And it's going to be parameterized

749
00:30:20,390 --> 00:30:22,140
by parameters theta, which are the weights

750
00:30:22,140 --> 00:30:24,140
and biases in a neural network.

751
00:30:24,140 --> 00:30:30,100
And think of that as assigning a likelihood, a probability,

752
00:30:30,100 --> 00:30:33,500
to every point along some data space X. So here,

753
00:30:33,500 --> 00:30:36,260
data is just a one-dimensional space.

754
00:30:36,260 --> 00:30:37,640
And here's my training data.

755
00:30:37,640 --> 00:30:40,640
My training data is examples of what the data looks like,

756
00:30:40,640 --> 00:30:42,540
and I want to make more like it.

757
00:30:42,540 --> 00:30:44,740
So in order to maximize the probability

758
00:30:44,740 --> 00:30:48,140
that my model P theta places on the training data,

759
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:49,420
I will start with some model.

760
00:30:49,420 --> 00:30:54,580
Maybe I have-- my neural network is going to output

761
00:30:54,580 --> 00:30:57,140
this shape, so it's going to output a structured object

762
00:30:57,140 --> 00:30:58,110
of this shape.

763
00:30:58,110 --> 00:30:59,730
Now, how does it actually do that?

764
00:30:59,730 --> 00:31:01,470
It's going to output the parameters

765
00:31:01,470 --> 00:31:03,510
of a parameterized function--

766
00:31:03,510 --> 00:31:06,110
like, a Gaussian is a parameterized function specified

767
00:31:06,110 --> 00:31:07,318
by the mean and the variance.

768
00:31:07,318 --> 00:31:09,152
So I could output the mean and the variance,

769
00:31:09,152 --> 00:31:11,950
and my neural net is telling me that that's the distribution it

770
00:31:11,950 --> 00:31:14,225
thinks explains the data.

771
00:31:14,225 --> 00:31:15,850
But it could be a mixture of Gaussians,

772
00:31:15,850 --> 00:31:17,430
or it could be any-- yeah, it could output, like,

773
00:31:17,430 --> 00:31:18,610
the shape of some spline.

774
00:31:18,610 --> 00:31:20,710
So the network can output a set of numbers that

775
00:31:20,710 --> 00:31:22,770
parameterizes the distribution.

776
00:31:22,770 --> 00:31:23,270
OK.

777
00:31:23,270 --> 00:31:25,650
So here's what I'll do with max likelihood modeling.

778
00:31:25,650 --> 00:31:30,190
I'll simply-- so the definition of a probability distribution

779
00:31:30,190 --> 00:31:32,910
is constant mass, right, so this area under the curve

780
00:31:32,910 --> 00:31:35,790
has to be constant, 1.

781
00:31:35,790 --> 00:31:36,370
OK.

782
00:31:36,370 --> 00:31:38,310
So I will push up the probability

783
00:31:38,310 --> 00:31:40,230
I place where the data lives.

784
00:31:40,230 --> 00:31:43,950
I'll increase the probability my model places on the data.

785
00:31:43,950 --> 00:31:46,350
And because it's constant mass, I'll

786
00:31:46,350 --> 00:31:48,870
have to take away mass from somewhere else, where

787
00:31:48,870 --> 00:31:51,270
the data does not live.

788
00:31:51,270 --> 00:31:54,870
OK, so I'm outputting a set of parameters

789
00:31:54,870 --> 00:31:57,680
that characterize a parametric family of distributions

790
00:31:57,680 --> 00:31:58,780
that integrate to 1.

791
00:31:58,780 --> 00:32:01,200
So every possible set of the parameters

792
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:05,920
specifies a curve, where the area under the curve is 1.

793
00:32:05,920 --> 00:32:08,740
And if I increase the curve where the data lives,

794
00:32:08,740 --> 00:32:12,720
I necessarily decrease the curve where the data does not live.

795
00:32:12,720 --> 00:32:16,940
And that's the idea of a density model.

796
00:32:16,940 --> 00:32:19,440
So a gradient update to my density model

797
00:32:19,440 --> 00:32:22,320
will increase the probability I place on the data

798
00:32:22,320 --> 00:32:26,000
and, therefore, decrease the probability a place elsewhere.

799
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:27,840
So the output of my neural network

800
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:31,740
is a set of parameters that characterize this curve.

801
00:32:31,740 --> 00:32:32,240
Yeah?

802
00:32:32,240 --> 00:32:33,027
Question.

803
00:32:33,027 --> 00:32:34,860
AUDIENCE: Can you go back one slide, please?

804
00:32:34,860 --> 00:32:35,760
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

805
00:32:35,760 --> 00:32:37,927
AUDIENCE: One thing I'm just a little confused about

806
00:32:37,927 --> 00:32:40,720
is you have p sub theta mapping each data

807
00:32:40,720 --> 00:32:44,660
point to some probability between 0 and infinity,

808
00:32:44,660 --> 00:32:47,400
but normally, I think of probability distributions

809
00:32:47,400 --> 00:32:48,140
as summing to 1.

810
00:32:48,140 --> 00:32:49,640
So it's hard to reconcile, like, how

811
00:32:49,640 --> 00:32:53,600
you can have points with very large probability, potentially.

812
00:32:53,600 --> 00:32:54,680
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

813
00:32:54,680 --> 00:32:59,960
So I think the question is, if the probability density can

814
00:32:59,960 --> 00:33:02,600
go to infinity, how can I-- like, how does that

815
00:33:02,600 --> 00:33:04,200
make sense numerically?

816
00:33:04,200 --> 00:33:06,120
How can that still integrate to 1?

817
00:33:06,120 --> 00:33:09,100
This is that kind of subtlety of continuous probabilities.

818
00:33:09,100 --> 00:33:13,000
We characterize the distribution with probability density

819
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,340
function, which is not actually a probability.

820
00:33:15,340 --> 00:33:18,510
The probability is an integral of the probability density

821
00:33:18,510 --> 00:33:20,760
over some interval, and that specifies the probability

822
00:33:20,760 --> 00:33:21,843
of being in that interval.

823
00:33:21,843 --> 00:33:23,520
And if the interval is infinitesimal,

824
00:33:23,520 --> 00:33:26,537
then the density can go to infinity over

825
00:33:26,537 --> 00:33:27,620
an infinitesimal interval.

826
00:33:27,620 --> 00:33:29,745
So it's like infinity divided by infinity, roughly.

827
00:33:29,745 --> 00:33:32,680
You get into the subtleties of continuous math.

828
00:33:32,680 --> 00:33:33,840
Yeah.

829
00:33:33,840 --> 00:33:36,280
OK.

830
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:37,200
Cool.

831
00:33:37,200 --> 00:33:45,040
So what is our objective going to be to fit my density to data?

832
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:46,220
I gave you the intuition.

833
00:33:46,220 --> 00:33:49,760
But formally, we like to minimize the difference

834
00:33:49,760 --> 00:33:54,653
between the world's true data generating distribution--

835
00:33:54,653 --> 00:33:56,570
so these data points, these black data points,

836
00:33:56,570 --> 00:33:58,310
came from some unknown process.

837
00:33:58,310 --> 00:34:00,492
We're going to call that unknown process pdata.

838
00:34:00,492 --> 00:34:02,950
We'll use that all over the place in the next few lectures.

839
00:34:02,950 --> 00:34:05,613
pdata is just the probability from which--

840
00:34:05,613 --> 00:34:08,030
the distribution from which the training data was sampled.

841
00:34:08,030 --> 00:34:08,988
But we don't know that.

842
00:34:08,988 --> 00:34:10,196
We don't have access to that.

843
00:34:10,196 --> 00:34:11,738
And then we're going to fit our model

844
00:34:11,738 --> 00:34:13,469
to be close to that distribution,

845
00:34:13,469 --> 00:34:15,270
according to the KL divergence.

846
00:34:15,270 --> 00:34:17,409
It's just a measure of the difference

847
00:34:17,409 --> 00:34:19,770
between two distributions.

848
00:34:19,770 --> 00:34:21,784
And we want our model distribution

849
00:34:21,784 --> 00:34:23,409
to be the same as nature's distribution

850
00:34:23,409 --> 00:34:25,730
that generated the training data.

851
00:34:25,730 --> 00:34:28,290
So we're going to see that it will come out

852
00:34:28,290 --> 00:34:31,949
to just pushing up probability where the data points live.

853
00:34:31,949 --> 00:34:33,870
But mathematically, here's what it looks like.

854
00:34:33,870 --> 00:34:36,969
So this is the definition of KL divergence.

855
00:34:36,969 --> 00:34:39,889
It'll be negative log of how much probability

856
00:34:39,889 --> 00:34:42,330
the model places on a data point, divided

857
00:34:42,330 --> 00:34:47,130
by how much probability nature placed on the data point.

858
00:34:47,130 --> 00:34:50,010
OK, so we want these to be the same.

859
00:34:50,010 --> 00:34:57,600
So we want these to go to 1, so that log of 1 will be 0.

860
00:34:57,600 --> 00:34:59,360
And then I will be minimizing this,

861
00:34:59,360 --> 00:35:02,980
and I'll eventually get these to be equal.

862
00:35:02,980 --> 00:35:04,980
OK.

863
00:35:04,980 --> 00:35:07,860
So we can separate out the log of the ratio

864
00:35:07,860 --> 00:35:11,820
into how much log probability I place on the data--

865
00:35:11,820 --> 00:35:13,900
sorry, how much log probability the model places

866
00:35:13,900 --> 00:35:16,540
on the data, minus how much log probability nature placed

867
00:35:16,540 --> 00:35:17,480
on the data.

868
00:35:17,480 --> 00:35:19,780
I want that difference to be 0.

869
00:35:19,780 --> 00:35:22,780
We can't actually measure how much probability nature placed

870
00:35:22,780 --> 00:35:23,280
on the data.

871
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:24,488
We don't have access to that.

872
00:35:24,488 --> 00:35:26,568
We only have access to samples from pdata.

873
00:35:26,568 --> 00:35:28,360
We don't have access to the function pdata.

874
00:35:28,360 --> 00:35:30,500
That's just the universe.

875
00:35:30,500 --> 00:35:31,620
OK.

876
00:35:31,620 --> 00:35:33,940
So the nice trick is that in order

877
00:35:33,940 --> 00:35:37,173
to maximize-- so, OK, we're minimizing the difference.

878
00:35:37,173 --> 00:35:38,840
But now we got rid of the negative sign,

879
00:35:38,840 --> 00:35:44,100
so going to maximize this term, this equation.

880
00:35:44,100 --> 00:35:46,300
Well, in order to maximize this with respect

881
00:35:46,300 --> 00:35:48,700
to the parameters theta, the parameters theta

882
00:35:48,700 --> 00:35:51,320
don't appear in the second term, so that's just a constant.

883
00:35:51,320 --> 00:35:53,020
So we don't actually have to consider

884
00:35:53,020 --> 00:35:54,560
that in the maximization.

885
00:35:54,560 --> 00:35:56,075
So we drop that second term.

886
00:35:56,075 --> 00:35:57,700
And that's great because we didn't even

887
00:35:57,700 --> 00:35:59,450
have a way of evaluating that second term,

888
00:35:59,450 --> 00:36:01,820
since we don't have access to pdata directly.

889
00:36:01,820 --> 00:36:04,060
And instead, we are going to maximize

890
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:07,260
the probability the model places on samples

891
00:36:07,260 --> 00:36:08,860
from the distribution.

892
00:36:08,860 --> 00:36:12,460
And whenever we try to maximize an expectation, in machine

893
00:36:12,460 --> 00:36:15,540
learning, we're going to tend to always approximate that

894
00:36:15,540 --> 00:36:18,540
with a finite set of samples.

895
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:22,430
So this is the probability the model places on the population.

896
00:36:22,430 --> 00:36:24,180
And this is the probability that the model

897
00:36:24,180 --> 00:36:26,580
places on the set of samples from the population, which

898
00:36:26,580 --> 00:36:28,060
are our training data.

899
00:36:28,060 --> 00:36:31,800
And this is called max likelihood learning--

900
00:36:31,800 --> 00:36:34,057
maximize the likelihood, the probability,

901
00:36:34,057 --> 00:36:36,140
that the model places on your training data, which

902
00:36:36,140 --> 00:36:41,460
are samples from some unknown data generating process, pdata.

903
00:36:41,460 --> 00:36:41,960
OK.

904
00:36:41,960 --> 00:36:44,220
So you should recognize this as being the same form

905
00:36:44,220 --> 00:36:50,172
as we saw with softmax regression, or it's kind of--

906
00:36:50,172 --> 00:36:51,630
max likelihood is usually the thing

907
00:36:51,630 --> 00:36:53,130
that we're doing in data modeling.

908
00:36:53,130 --> 00:36:55,270
It's often the thing we're doing.

909
00:36:55,270 --> 00:36:56,470
OK.

910
00:36:56,470 --> 00:36:56,970
Yeah?

911
00:36:56,970 --> 00:36:57,527
Question.

912
00:36:57,527 --> 00:36:59,610
AUDIENCE: If you don't have a regularization term,

913
00:36:59,610 --> 00:37:01,110
is this just going to lead to, like,

914
00:37:01,110 --> 00:37:03,478
Dirac delta spikes on your data points?

915
00:37:03,478 --> 00:37:04,270
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes.

916
00:37:04,270 --> 00:37:04,570
OK.

917
00:37:04,570 --> 00:37:06,528
If you don't have a regularizer, will this just

918
00:37:06,528 --> 00:37:09,390
lead to Dirac delta function on your data points?

919
00:37:09,390 --> 00:37:11,070
That's going to be the next slide.

920
00:37:11,070 --> 00:37:11,610
Wonderful.

921
00:37:11,610 --> 00:37:14,212
So this is the question that comes up all the time

922
00:37:14,212 --> 00:37:15,170
in generative modeling.

923
00:37:15,170 --> 00:37:16,670
It's kind of confusing.

924
00:37:16,670 --> 00:37:18,030
So here's a question for you.

925
00:37:18,030 --> 00:37:20,550
Is the filing cabinet-- remember the filing cabinet

926
00:37:20,550 --> 00:37:22,230
from the lecture on generalization?

927
00:37:22,230 --> 00:37:25,190
Is the filing cabinet a good generative model?

928
00:37:25,190 --> 00:37:27,270
OK, so what is the filing cabinet?

929
00:37:27,270 --> 00:37:29,570
I'm going to define it as follows.

930
00:37:29,570 --> 00:37:33,170
I will train the generative model by taking my data set X,

931
00:37:33,170 --> 00:37:37,750
and I'll just put every data point into a file.

932
00:37:37,750 --> 00:37:39,490
So I'll just add to the cabinet.

933
00:37:39,490 --> 00:37:41,710
And then to sample from my learned generative model,

934
00:37:41,710 --> 00:37:45,680
this cabinet, I will simply take a random drawer, or random file,

935
00:37:45,680 --> 00:37:47,360
and pull it out.

936
00:37:47,360 --> 00:37:50,180
So is this a good generative model.

937
00:37:50,180 --> 00:37:52,830
What do you think?

938
00:37:52,830 --> 00:37:54,330
AUDIENCE: It doesn't make new stuff.

939
00:37:54,330 --> 00:37:56,038
PHILLIP ISOLA: It doesn't make new stuff.

940
00:37:56,038 --> 00:37:57,832
OK.

941
00:37:57,832 --> 00:38:02,085
But it does make data which is identical to the training data.

942
00:38:02,085 --> 00:38:04,460
So that's like-- that kind of seemed like what we wanted.

943
00:38:04,460 --> 00:38:07,110
We wanted to make data that's identical to the training data,

944
00:38:07,110 --> 00:38:08,120
right?

945
00:38:08,120 --> 00:38:10,000
This does maximize the likelihood.

946
00:38:10,000 --> 00:38:11,972
This is the max likelihood solution,

947
00:38:11,972 --> 00:38:13,680
or this is getting the highest likelihood

948
00:38:13,680 --> 00:38:15,200
you could possibly get.

949
00:38:15,200 --> 00:38:19,200
Just memorize the data and output the same data.

950
00:38:19,200 --> 00:38:21,520
You're right, but any other answers?

951
00:38:21,520 --> 00:38:24,200
Want to elaborate on this?

952
00:38:24,200 --> 00:38:25,940
How could we avoid this solution?

953
00:38:25,940 --> 00:38:28,060
This is intuitively a bad solution.

954
00:38:28,060 --> 00:38:31,000
I didn't do anything.

955
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:31,820
Any thoughts?

956
00:38:34,440 --> 00:38:35,160
OK.

957
00:38:35,160 --> 00:38:37,560
So here's how I like to think about it.

958
00:38:37,560 --> 00:38:40,200
It's exactly the same as overfitting in classical machine

959
00:38:40,200 --> 00:38:40,700
learning.

960
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:42,120
So if you understand overfitting,

961
00:38:42,120 --> 00:38:44,490
then you can understand why the filing cabinet

962
00:38:44,490 --> 00:38:46,250
is a bad generative model.

963
00:38:46,250 --> 00:38:48,290
So here's my training data.

964
00:38:48,290 --> 00:38:53,290
And exactly as you said, the optimal generative model--

965
00:38:53,290 --> 00:38:55,150
and the max likelihood generative model,

966
00:38:55,150 --> 00:38:57,462
if I have infinite capacity, I can

967
00:38:57,462 --> 00:39:00,170
fit any wiggly curve-- is to put a delta function on all the data

968
00:39:00,170 --> 00:39:02,690
points, on all the training points.

969
00:39:02,690 --> 00:39:05,090
But the true data generating process

970
00:39:05,090 --> 00:39:07,570
is something unknown, which here, as a cartoon,

971
00:39:07,570 --> 00:39:08,850
it could look like that.

972
00:39:08,850 --> 00:39:11,890
So these blue dots are just samples, my training data

973
00:39:11,890 --> 00:39:14,250
samples, from some underlying process.

974
00:39:14,250 --> 00:39:16,030
So what went wrong here?

975
00:39:16,030 --> 00:39:19,270
If I take new samples from that same process,

976
00:39:19,270 --> 00:39:20,510
they'll land somewhere else.

977
00:39:20,510 --> 00:39:22,890
And I'll have placed zero probability, under this model,

978
00:39:22,890 --> 00:39:24,410
on those samples.

979
00:39:24,410 --> 00:39:27,730
So what matters in generative modeling is the probability

980
00:39:27,730 --> 00:39:31,130
you place on the test data, on new samples from the data

981
00:39:31,130 --> 00:39:33,010
generating process.

982
00:39:33,010 --> 00:39:35,535
We optimize the probability you place on the training data,

983
00:39:35,535 --> 00:39:37,410
but what matters is the probability you place

984
00:39:37,410 --> 00:39:39,130
on the test data-- exactly the same

985
00:39:39,130 --> 00:39:42,100
as overfitting in machine learning, where you actually

986
00:39:42,100 --> 00:39:44,350
care about test performance, not training performance.

987
00:39:44,350 --> 00:39:46,010
But here, test performance is going

988
00:39:46,010 --> 00:39:49,210
to be the density you have placed on new samples

989
00:39:49,210 --> 00:39:51,370
from the true generating process.

990
00:39:51,370 --> 00:39:54,610
And you have to control capacity or regularize in order

991
00:39:54,610 --> 00:39:58,690
to avoid the memorization solution.

992
00:39:58,690 --> 00:40:00,908
OK.

993
00:40:00,908 --> 00:40:03,450
So that's what overfitting looks like for a generative model.

994
00:40:03,450 --> 00:40:04,825
So the goal of a generative model

995
00:40:04,825 --> 00:40:07,210
is not to make exactly the training data

996
00:40:07,210 --> 00:40:10,210
or, actually, maximize likelihood on the training data.

997
00:40:10,210 --> 00:40:12,850
It's to make things that are new but capture

998
00:40:12,850 --> 00:40:15,170
the important statistics and realistic properties

999
00:40:15,170 --> 00:40:18,250
of the data, but are infinitely new.

1000
00:40:18,250 --> 00:40:20,490
We can quantify that as likelihood of the test data

1001
00:40:20,490 --> 00:40:21,930
under a model.

1002
00:40:21,930 --> 00:40:25,350
Now, if I don't have an explicit representation of the density,

1003
00:40:25,350 --> 00:40:27,230
then it's not easy to quantify this.

1004
00:40:27,230 --> 00:40:29,950
And that's why these methods, the direct methods,

1005
00:40:29,950 --> 00:40:31,850
that just produce samples but not densities,

1006
00:40:31,850 --> 00:40:33,568
are a little bit hard to calibrate.

1007
00:40:33,568 --> 00:40:35,610
It's a little bit hard to know what the test data

1008
00:40:35,610 --> 00:40:37,010
likelihood is.

1009
00:40:37,010 --> 00:40:38,540
But with density models, I can just

1010
00:40:38,540 --> 00:40:40,020
cross-validate by having a test set

1011
00:40:40,020 --> 00:40:43,300
and measuring the likelihood I place on the test set.

1012
00:40:43,300 --> 00:40:45,340
OK.

1013
00:40:45,340 --> 00:40:49,278
So here's the way that you can measure the quality

1014
00:40:49,278 --> 00:40:50,320
of your generative model.

1015
00:40:50,320 --> 00:40:51,000
You can evaluate it.

1016
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:51,917
You can't fit on this.

1017
00:40:51,917 --> 00:40:54,460
This is like the test score, the validation score.

1018
00:40:54,460 --> 00:40:57,040
You sample new samples from the world.

1019
00:40:57,040 --> 00:40:59,680
You make a split, your test split or your validation split.

1020
00:40:59,680 --> 00:41:01,260
And you measure generalization error

1021
00:41:01,260 --> 00:41:05,080
as the likelihood your model places on the test data.

1022
00:41:05,080 --> 00:41:05,580
OK.

1023
00:41:08,940 --> 00:41:09,440
OK.

1024
00:41:09,440 --> 00:41:11,780
Any questions about density models?

1025
00:41:11,780 --> 00:41:15,860
And then we're going to do energy models.

1026
00:41:15,860 --> 00:41:17,060
Yeah?

1027
00:41:17,060 --> 00:41:19,800
AUDIENCE: How exactly is p sub theta parameterized?

1028
00:41:19,800 --> 00:41:22,700
Like what is it actually doing?

1029
00:41:22,700 --> 00:41:25,795
PHILLIP ISOLA: So theta, how is it parameterized?

1030
00:41:25,795 --> 00:41:27,920
You can parameterize it in a lot of different ways.

1031
00:41:27,920 --> 00:41:30,020
So one example would be the network

1032
00:41:30,020 --> 00:41:32,420
outputs two numbers, two scalars, so it's

1033
00:41:32,420 --> 00:41:33,820
a two-dimensional vector.

1034
00:41:33,820 --> 00:41:35,820
That would be sufficient to parameterize

1035
00:41:35,820 --> 00:41:37,522
a one-dimensional Gaussian.

1036
00:41:37,522 --> 00:41:38,730
The first number is the mean.

1037
00:41:38,730 --> 00:41:40,590
The second number is the variance.

1038
00:41:40,590 --> 00:41:43,930
Another option is the network could output a thousand numbers,

1039
00:41:43,930 --> 00:41:46,710
and that would be sufficient to characterize--

1040
00:41:46,710 --> 00:41:48,150
or, let's say, a hundred numbers,

1041
00:41:48,150 --> 00:41:50,070
or a hundred plus 10 numbers, that would be sufficient

1042
00:41:50,070 --> 00:41:52,510
to characterize a 10-dimensional Gaussian where I have

1043
00:41:52,510 --> 00:41:55,970
a covariance matrix which is 10 by 10, so a hundred numbers,

1044
00:41:55,970 --> 00:41:57,870
and a mean which is 10 dimensions.

1045
00:41:57,870 --> 00:42:01,070
So I have to output the number of--

1046
00:42:01,070 --> 00:42:02,790
the vector output by my network has

1047
00:42:02,790 --> 00:42:05,710
to be all the parameters of the family of distributions

1048
00:42:05,710 --> 00:42:07,790
that I am considering.

1049
00:42:07,790 --> 00:42:09,770
And if I want to do a mixture of Gaussians,

1050
00:42:09,770 --> 00:42:12,170
then I have to-- if I want to output two Gaussians,

1051
00:42:12,170 --> 00:42:15,930
I can do that with 2 times 100 plus 10 numbers.

1052
00:42:15,930 --> 00:42:18,850
And if I wanted to output other, funny-shaped curves,

1053
00:42:18,850 --> 00:42:21,195
I can do that with other parametric families.

1054
00:42:21,195 --> 00:42:22,570
It doesn't have to be a Gaussian.

1055
00:42:22,570 --> 00:42:25,950
But that's the general idea.

1056
00:42:25,950 --> 00:42:30,270
AUDIENCE: So assuming that you have a finite set of validation

1057
00:42:30,270 --> 00:42:30,873
data--

1058
00:42:30,873 --> 00:42:32,790
I assume your procedure would be to train this

1059
00:42:32,790 --> 00:42:36,030
until your validation loss starts to increase,

1060
00:42:36,030 --> 00:42:38,310
and then you do early stopping to try and find

1061
00:42:38,310 --> 00:42:41,822
a good point at which you've got a good generative model.

1062
00:42:41,822 --> 00:42:44,030
Is there an analogy similar to what we learned before

1063
00:42:44,030 --> 00:42:46,870
about inductive bias in these models?

1064
00:42:46,870 --> 00:42:49,870
Presumably, if you're trying to generate images,

1065
00:42:49,870 --> 00:42:51,630
you should somehow be able to add

1066
00:42:51,630 --> 00:42:53,750
a form of bias or regularization that says,

1067
00:42:53,750 --> 00:42:56,910
like, images should not look like static.

1068
00:42:56,910 --> 00:43:00,550
You should not be able to have just a zero-value pixel

1069
00:43:00,550 --> 00:43:02,790
next to something that's a max-value pixel,

1070
00:43:02,790 --> 00:43:04,950
so images have this sort of smooth quality.

1071
00:43:04,950 --> 00:43:08,370
So does that show up in how you regularize in the loss?

1072
00:43:08,370 --> 00:43:10,110
Or is that a [AUDIO OUT]?

1073
00:43:10,110 --> 00:43:11,110
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1074
00:43:11,110 --> 00:43:12,210
So yeah, great question.

1075
00:43:12,210 --> 00:43:16,190
So can we add inductive biases to generative models

1076
00:43:16,190 --> 00:43:18,218
to prevent them from finding bad solutions

1077
00:43:18,218 --> 00:43:20,510
and kind of encourage them to find a good solution that

1078
00:43:20,510 --> 00:43:22,630
actually generalizes to the test data?

1079
00:43:22,630 --> 00:43:25,590
And all the tricks that we have from other deep learning

1080
00:43:25,590 --> 00:43:26,610
can apply here.

1081
00:43:26,610 --> 00:43:29,190
So you can use an architecture which

1082
00:43:29,190 --> 00:43:31,910
has some kind of spatial smoothness.

1083
00:43:31,910 --> 00:43:35,800
So a convolutional architecture has baked-in spatial locality.

1084
00:43:35,800 --> 00:43:37,680
You can use positional codes in transformers

1085
00:43:37,680 --> 00:43:39,480
that have baked-in kind of inductive bias

1086
00:43:39,480 --> 00:43:41,080
towards spatial locality.

1087
00:43:41,080 --> 00:43:44,040
You can use regularizers that-- you can

1088
00:43:44,040 --> 00:43:45,280
do all the tricks, basically.

1089
00:43:45,280 --> 00:43:48,320
There's a lot to say, but you can do everything.

1090
00:43:48,320 --> 00:43:49,600
Yeah.

1091
00:43:49,600 --> 00:43:50,440
OK.

1092
00:43:50,440 --> 00:43:53,227
Question in the back, and then I'll move on.

1093
00:43:53,227 --> 00:43:55,562
AUDIENCE: Is there a connection between max likelihood

1094
00:43:55,562 --> 00:43:58,287
estimation and empirical risk minimization?

1095
00:43:58,287 --> 00:43:59,120
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1096
00:43:59,120 --> 00:44:01,412
Is there a connection between max likelihood estimation

1097
00:44:01,412 --> 00:44:02,920
and empirical risk minimization?

1098
00:44:02,920 --> 00:44:04,860
Generally, yes.

1099
00:44:04,860 --> 00:44:09,168
I guess I would say empirical max likelihood,

1100
00:44:09,168 --> 00:44:10,960
which is the maximized likelihood you place

1101
00:44:10,960 --> 00:44:12,920
on the data-- that's what empirical means,

1102
00:44:12,920 --> 00:44:16,240
is on the training data--

1103
00:44:16,240 --> 00:44:18,960
that is a form of empirical risk minimization for the risk

1104
00:44:18,960 --> 00:44:20,860
function which is the likelihood of the data.

1105
00:44:20,860 --> 00:44:22,360
But you could have more general risk

1106
00:44:22,360 --> 00:44:25,840
functions that might not have interpretations as likelihood.

1107
00:44:25,840 --> 00:44:26,400
Yeah.

1108
00:44:26,400 --> 00:44:30,620
But typically, the ones we use, like squared error or L1 error,

1109
00:44:30,620 --> 00:44:32,850
these all have interpretations as some type

1110
00:44:32,850 --> 00:44:37,090
of likelihood function.

1111
00:44:37,090 --> 00:44:39,710
So let's talk about energy-based models.

1112
00:44:39,710 --> 00:44:41,770
So energy-based models are--

1113
00:44:41,770 --> 00:44:43,810
I said that the indirect approach-- this

1114
00:44:43,810 --> 00:44:45,630
is another possible indirect approach.

1115
00:44:45,630 --> 00:44:49,110
The indirect approach is you try to score your data

1116
00:44:49,110 --> 00:44:51,870
and then sample from or optimize the score,

1117
00:44:51,870 --> 00:44:56,638
find data points that have high score.

1118
00:44:56,638 --> 00:44:58,930
Energy-based models say, Well, what if the score is not

1119
00:44:58,930 --> 00:45:02,650
a probability, but it's just an unnormalized probability?

1120
00:45:02,650 --> 00:45:05,930
So remember that the definition of a probability density

1121
00:45:05,930 --> 00:45:08,010
is it integrates to 1.

1122
00:45:08,010 --> 00:45:11,810
An energy function is just a probability

1123
00:45:11,810 --> 00:45:13,270
that is unnormalized.

1124
00:45:13,270 --> 00:45:18,690
So for any probability, I can represent-- or, yeah,

1125
00:45:18,690 --> 00:45:21,943
for a broad family of functions, I can represent them like this.

1126
00:45:21,943 --> 00:45:24,110
This is sometimes called the Boltzmann distribution.

1127
00:45:24,110 --> 00:45:26,850
So it's an exponential of the negative energy

1128
00:45:26,850 --> 00:45:29,700
divided by a normalization.

1129
00:45:29,700 --> 00:45:34,180
And any energy function, any function E,

1130
00:45:34,180 --> 00:45:37,140
that is not necessarily normalized,

1131
00:45:37,140 --> 00:45:40,753
will, through this map, imply a probability function

1132
00:45:40,753 --> 00:45:43,420
p that is normalized, because I just divide by the normalization

1133
00:45:43,420 --> 00:45:44,980
constant.

1134
00:45:44,980 --> 00:45:48,540
So whatever E is, if I divide by Z, which is this integral,

1135
00:45:48,540 --> 00:45:50,960
p will be normalized.

1136
00:45:50,960 --> 00:45:51,460
Yeah?

1137
00:45:51,460 --> 00:45:53,117
AUDIENCE: What is an energy function?

1138
00:45:53,117 --> 00:45:54,700
PHILLIP ISOLA: Energy function is just

1139
00:45:54,700 --> 00:45:58,020
a map from data to a scalar, between negative infinity

1140
00:45:58,020 --> 00:45:58,960
and infinity.

1141
00:45:58,960 --> 00:46:01,095
AUDIENCE: So kind of like a loss, in a sense?

1142
00:46:01,095 --> 00:46:03,220
PHILLIP ISOLA: Just like the score-- so think of it

1143
00:46:03,220 --> 00:46:04,838
as an unnormalized probability.

1144
00:46:04,838 --> 00:46:05,380
AUDIENCE: OK.

1145
00:46:05,380 --> 00:46:05,700
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1146
00:46:05,700 --> 00:46:07,140
Unnormalized probability.

1147
00:46:07,140 --> 00:46:07,727
Yeah.

1148
00:46:07,727 --> 00:46:10,060
AUDIENCE: Kind of like the thing that goes into softmax?

1149
00:46:10,060 --> 00:46:10,852
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes.

1150
00:46:10,852 --> 00:46:12,980
So the logits in a softmax classifier

1151
00:46:12,980 --> 00:46:15,980
can be considered the energy.

1152
00:46:15,980 --> 00:46:17,900
And then, softmax is just, you divide

1153
00:46:17,900 --> 00:46:19,858
by the normalization-- you exponentiate and you

1154
00:46:19,858 --> 00:46:21,580
divide by the normalization.

1155
00:46:21,580 --> 00:46:23,620
OK.

1156
00:46:23,620 --> 00:46:24,120
OK.

1157
00:46:24,120 --> 00:46:26,620
So why are energies good?

1158
00:46:26,620 --> 00:46:28,460
Because they're often sufficient.

1159
00:46:28,460 --> 00:46:31,320
So, OK, so first of all, why are probabilities bad?

1160
00:46:31,320 --> 00:46:33,840
Because probabilities need to be normalized.

1161
00:46:33,840 --> 00:46:35,820
So my family of functions I search over

1162
00:46:35,820 --> 00:46:37,660
is just normalized functions.

1163
00:46:37,660 --> 00:46:40,220
And if I search over arbitrary curves-- like,

1164
00:46:40,220 --> 00:46:42,660
let's say the neural network doesn't parameterize

1165
00:46:42,660 --> 00:46:45,020
a Gaussian, which is normalized, but it just

1166
00:46:45,020 --> 00:46:49,245
outputs some polynomial curve.

1167
00:46:49,245 --> 00:46:51,620
Or maybe it just outputs a set of numbers that are, like,

1168
00:46:51,620 --> 00:46:52,920
the control points on a spline.

1169
00:46:52,920 --> 00:46:54,860
Whatever.

1170
00:46:54,860 --> 00:46:58,580
Or maybe it just outputs, like, a number per data point.

1171
00:46:58,580 --> 00:47:00,413
So I could have a network that takes in data

1172
00:47:00,413 --> 00:47:02,538
and just outputs a number between negative infinity

1173
00:47:02,538 --> 00:47:03,140
and infinity.

1174
00:47:03,140 --> 00:47:04,528
Well, how would I normalize that?

1175
00:47:04,528 --> 00:47:06,820
I'd have to do this integral over all possible settings

1176
00:47:06,820 --> 00:47:07,320
of the data.

1177
00:47:07,320 --> 00:47:08,278
And that's intractable.

1178
00:47:08,278 --> 00:47:09,460
I don't want to do that.

1179
00:47:09,460 --> 00:47:12,220
So that's why probability densities are bad,

1180
00:47:12,220 --> 00:47:15,200
because I have to consider families that are normalized,

1181
00:47:15,200 --> 00:47:17,500
and that's very restrictive.

1182
00:47:17,500 --> 00:47:20,380
But energies are sufficient because energies,

1183
00:47:20,380 --> 00:47:24,000
energy ratios are equal to probability ratios OK.

1184
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:26,270
So the probability that I place on-- let's say

1185
00:47:26,270 --> 00:47:27,877
I want to say, Which is more likely,

1186
00:47:27,877 --> 00:47:29,710
that it's going to snow tomorrow or there'll

1187
00:47:29,710 --> 00:47:30,990
be a hurricane tomorrow?

1188
00:47:30,990 --> 00:47:32,830
So that's some probability ratio.

1189
00:47:32,830 --> 00:47:34,470
And maybe that's the type of thing

1190
00:47:34,470 --> 00:47:38,732
I need to decide for how to prepare for some disaster.

1191
00:47:38,732 --> 00:47:40,690
Oftentimes, relative judgments are what matter.

1192
00:47:40,690 --> 00:47:42,815
And so relative judgments, the relative probability

1193
00:47:42,815 --> 00:47:46,390
of x1 divided by x2, is equal to-- here's

1194
00:47:46,390 --> 00:47:50,030
the definition of my energy function.

1195
00:47:50,030 --> 00:47:53,070
And notice that the normalization functions

1196
00:47:53,070 --> 00:47:54,430
cancel out.

1197
00:47:54,430 --> 00:48:00,030
So that means that exponential energies provide the same ratio

1198
00:48:00,030 --> 00:48:02,390
as probabilities.

1199
00:48:02,390 --> 00:48:06,710
And I can evaluate whether a hurricane

1200
00:48:06,710 --> 00:48:08,730
is less likely or more likely than snow

1201
00:48:08,730 --> 00:48:10,230
by just looking at the energy ratio,

1202
00:48:10,230 --> 00:48:12,750
as opposed to the probability ratio, which is intractable

1203
00:48:12,750 --> 00:48:16,010
unless I know the normalization.

1204
00:48:16,010 --> 00:48:16,510
Yeah?

1205
00:48:16,510 --> 00:48:18,110
AUDIENCE: So it's the energy ratio?

1206
00:48:18,110 --> 00:48:19,510
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1207
00:48:19,510 --> 00:48:22,130
AUDIENCE: So I guess energy ration includes exponentiation.

1208
00:48:22,130 --> 00:48:22,963
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1209
00:48:22,963 --> 00:48:25,333
AUDIENCE: So it's quantum difference, or?

1210
00:48:25,333 --> 00:48:27,000
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, you could do like--

1211
00:48:27,000 --> 00:48:28,200
so the question is like--

1212
00:48:28,200 --> 00:48:29,540
I said energy ratio.

1213
00:48:29,540 --> 00:48:32,185
I meant exponential. e to the negative energy divided

1214
00:48:32,185 --> 00:48:33,560
by e to the negative other energy

1215
00:48:33,560 --> 00:48:34,980
is equal to the probability ratio.

1216
00:48:34,980 --> 00:48:36,760
If you took a log of that, you could see,

1217
00:48:36,760 --> 00:48:38,080
it'll have another form.

1218
00:48:38,080 --> 00:48:39,520
It'll be some difference.

1219
00:48:39,520 --> 00:48:41,440
OK.

1220
00:48:41,440 --> 00:48:44,480
So this is-- relative probabilities are often all

1221
00:48:44,480 --> 00:48:45,840
you need for decisionmaking.

1222
00:48:45,840 --> 00:48:49,900
In particular, they're also all you need for a lot of samplers.

1223
00:48:49,900 --> 00:48:53,260
So Markov chain Monte Carlo only requires relative probabilities,

1224
00:48:53,260 --> 00:48:56,260
so you don't need to have a normalized density or output.

1225
00:48:56,260 --> 00:48:59,960
You can just output arbitrary unnormalized numbers and still

1226
00:48:59,960 --> 00:49:02,360
sample from that, the implied distribution.

1227
00:49:02,360 --> 00:49:02,860
OK.

1228
00:49:02,860 --> 00:49:05,960
So energy functions are often all you need.

1229
00:49:05,960 --> 00:49:06,800
Yeah?

1230
00:49:06,800 --> 00:49:08,592
AUDIENCE: Can you explain the negative sign

1231
00:49:08,592 --> 00:49:09,680
in front of the energy?

1232
00:49:09,680 --> 00:49:11,160
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so that's just a definition.

1233
00:49:11,160 --> 00:49:12,780
You could have-- you don't need that.

1234
00:49:12,780 --> 00:49:15,320
It's just the definition.

1235
00:49:15,320 --> 00:49:19,280
Because E is just a function that outputs negative infinity

1236
00:49:19,280 --> 00:49:23,040
to infinity, so the definition is energies

1237
00:49:23,040 --> 00:49:25,020
are related to negative probabilities.

1238
00:49:25,020 --> 00:49:27,060
So low energy means high probability.

1239
00:49:27,060 --> 00:49:30,680
It's just a definition.

1240
00:49:30,680 --> 00:49:32,510
OK.

1241
00:49:32,510 --> 00:49:34,620
So why would you actually want a probability?

1242
00:49:34,620 --> 00:49:36,580
Why wouldn't you always work with energies?

1243
00:49:36,580 --> 00:49:39,720
Anyone have some thoughts on that?

1244
00:49:39,720 --> 00:49:42,700
Why would I ever care about using normalized probabilities,

1245
00:49:42,700 --> 00:49:46,460
if what I'm saying is true?

1246
00:49:50,760 --> 00:49:51,940
Yeah, any thoughts on that?

1247
00:49:51,940 --> 00:49:52,440
Yeah?

1248
00:49:52,440 --> 00:49:54,815
AUDIENCE: How would you scale them to different concepts,

1249
00:49:54,815 --> 00:49:55,440
perhaps?

1250
00:49:55,440 --> 00:49:56,760
PHILLIP ISOLA: How would you scale them?

1251
00:49:56,760 --> 00:49:57,940
AUDIENCE: To different concepts.

1252
00:49:57,940 --> 00:49:59,020
PHILLIP ISOLA: To different concepts.

1253
00:49:59,020 --> 00:49:59,617
Yeah.

1254
00:49:59,617 --> 00:50:01,700
So how would you scale them to different concepts?

1255
00:50:01,700 --> 00:50:04,600
I think that's getting at what I think I agree with,

1256
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:07,600
that probabilities put everything

1257
00:50:07,600 --> 00:50:11,562
on the same kind of unit, on the same playing field.

1258
00:50:11,562 --> 00:50:14,020
Like, you're kind of scaling things to have the same units.

1259
00:50:14,020 --> 00:50:17,200
So 90% probability could be--

1260
00:50:17,200 --> 00:50:19,710
in one domain, it's 90% probability in another domain,

1261
00:50:19,710 --> 00:50:22,370
but the energies could be arbitrarily scaled

1262
00:50:22,370 --> 00:50:24,170
or arbitrarily offset.

1263
00:50:24,170 --> 00:50:26,530
So I think probabilities are often

1264
00:50:26,530 --> 00:50:28,330
more interpretable and more exchangeable

1265
00:50:28,330 --> 00:50:29,673
between different problems.

1266
00:50:29,673 --> 00:50:31,090
If you go to the hospital and they

1267
00:50:31,090 --> 00:50:33,320
tell you your energy of having cancer is 10 million,

1268
00:50:33,320 --> 00:50:35,070
what are you going to do with that, right?

1269
00:50:35,070 --> 00:50:37,930
But if they say your likelihood is 0.1%,

1270
00:50:37,930 --> 00:50:40,170
you're going to be like, OK, that's fine.

1271
00:50:40,170 --> 00:50:42,040
OK.

1272
00:50:42,040 --> 00:50:44,150
So human interpretability is the most obvious,

1273
00:50:44,150 --> 00:50:47,330
but I think there's other reasons beyond that, too.

1274
00:50:47,330 --> 00:50:49,310
So how do you optimize an energy-based model?

1275
00:50:49,310 --> 00:50:51,490
How do you fit an energy-based model to data?

1276
00:50:51,490 --> 00:50:52,987
So here's how you do it.

1277
00:50:52,987 --> 00:50:54,570
So we do the same thing we did before.

1278
00:50:54,570 --> 00:50:58,570
We find our data and we decrease the energy where the data lives.

1279
00:50:58,570 --> 00:51:01,530
Remember, decrease energy means increase probability,

1280
00:51:01,530 --> 00:51:04,490
because energy is related to probability by negative.

1281
00:51:04,490 --> 00:51:08,410
e to the negative energy is probability.

1282
00:51:08,410 --> 00:51:09,290
OK.

1283
00:51:09,290 --> 00:51:12,030
So what's wrong with this?

1284
00:51:12,030 --> 00:51:15,650
We're going to decrease our energy where the data lives.

1285
00:51:15,650 --> 00:51:18,460
What's wrong with this?

1286
00:51:18,460 --> 00:51:20,020
What's wrong with this?

1287
00:51:20,020 --> 00:51:21,305
AUDIENCE: It's not constant.

1288
00:51:21,305 --> 00:51:22,180
PHILLIP ISOLA: Sorry.

1289
00:51:22,180 --> 00:51:22,680
Yes.

1290
00:51:22,680 --> 00:51:23,980
Not constant mass.

1291
00:51:23,980 --> 00:51:25,940
What's wrong with this is I could just

1292
00:51:25,940 --> 00:51:28,960
decrease my energy to be negative infinity everywhere.

1293
00:51:28,960 --> 00:51:31,440
If my only goal is decrease energy where the data lives,

1294
00:51:31,440 --> 00:51:34,140
I can just say, well, I'll also decrease energy everywhere else.

1295
00:51:34,140 --> 00:51:36,480
I'm not forcing it to be normalized.

1296
00:51:36,480 --> 00:51:39,460
So there's no necessity to increase energy

1297
00:51:39,460 --> 00:51:41,220
where the data does not live.

1298
00:51:41,220 --> 00:51:45,060
So the simple solution is I will add a term to increase energy

1299
00:51:45,060 --> 00:51:47,900
where the data doesn't live.

1300
00:51:47,900 --> 00:51:52,580
So the way you do this is you decrease energy

1301
00:51:52,580 --> 00:51:56,220
where the data lives and you increase energy where

1302
00:51:56,220 --> 00:51:57,960
the model places high energy.

1303
00:51:57,960 --> 00:51:59,640
You actually sample from the model.

1304
00:51:59,640 --> 00:52:02,560
So I said you can sample from an energy--

1305
00:52:02,560 --> 00:52:04,060
you can sample from the distribution

1306
00:52:04,060 --> 00:52:06,300
implied by an energy without having the normalization

1307
00:52:06,300 --> 00:52:06,920
constant.

1308
00:52:06,920 --> 00:52:10,900
That's what things like Markov chain Monte Carlo do.

1309
00:52:10,900 --> 00:52:13,400
So I can sample from my energy function,

1310
00:52:13,400 --> 00:52:16,050
but I can't evaluate the probability.

1311
00:52:16,050 --> 00:52:18,033
But I can take these points, and I'm

1312
00:52:18,033 --> 00:52:19,950
sampling where there's low energy because that

1313
00:52:19,950 --> 00:52:21,110
means high probability.

1314
00:52:21,110 --> 00:52:23,150
I'm going to increase energy there,

1315
00:52:23,150 --> 00:52:25,430
and I'll get this solution.

1316
00:52:25,430 --> 00:52:27,990
If I repeat this process, I decrease energy

1317
00:52:27,990 --> 00:52:30,670
where the data lives and I increase energy

1318
00:52:30,670 --> 00:52:32,390
where my current energy function implies

1319
00:52:32,390 --> 00:52:35,110
high probability via sampling.

1320
00:52:35,110 --> 00:52:36,490
I go like that.

1321
00:52:36,490 --> 00:52:37,730
And eventually, I get this.

1322
00:52:37,730 --> 00:52:40,490
And here, I decrease energy where the data lives,

1323
00:52:40,490 --> 00:52:42,390
and I increase energy where the model

1324
00:52:42,390 --> 00:52:44,870
has placed high probability.

1325
00:52:44,870 --> 00:52:46,050
And they cancel out.

1326
00:52:46,050 --> 00:52:49,330
So eventually, I get a delta function on the data points.

1327
00:52:49,330 --> 00:52:52,400
And that convergence of this algorithm, the positive term,

1328
00:52:52,400 --> 00:52:54,650
which is decrease in energy, equals the negative term,

1329
00:52:54,650 --> 00:52:57,390
which is increasing energy, because the model places energy

1330
00:52:57,390 --> 00:52:58,730
exactly where the data lives.

1331
00:52:58,730 --> 00:52:59,230
OK.

1332
00:52:59,230 --> 00:53:01,750
So that should, therefore, kind convince you graphically

1333
00:53:01,750 --> 00:53:05,170
that this thing will converge to a solution that fits the data.

1334
00:53:05,170 --> 00:53:07,670
It will overfit in the same way that other generative models

1335
00:53:07,670 --> 00:53:08,830
overfit.

1336
00:53:08,830 --> 00:53:11,830
But we'll just do early stopping or regularization

1337
00:53:11,830 --> 00:53:13,370
of various forms.

1338
00:53:13,370 --> 00:53:13,870
OK.

1339
00:53:13,870 --> 00:53:17,750
So at convergence, the green arrows and the red arrows

1340
00:53:17,750 --> 00:53:18,950
cancel out.

1341
00:53:18,950 --> 00:53:21,610
I get zero delta between them, and the thing stops.

1342
00:53:21,610 --> 00:53:23,170
So let's look at that mathematically.

1343
00:53:23,170 --> 00:53:25,210
This algorithm is called contrastive divergence.

1344
00:53:25,210 --> 00:53:27,117
And variations on this come up a lot.

1345
00:53:27,117 --> 00:53:28,950
It's even connected to contrastive learning,

1346
00:53:28,950 --> 00:53:32,150
but a little bit indirectly.

1347
00:53:32,150 --> 00:53:33,830
It's another contrast, the contrast

1348
00:53:33,830 --> 00:53:36,710
between where the data lives and where the model has

1349
00:53:36,710 --> 00:53:37,730
placed high probability.

1350
00:53:37,730 --> 00:53:40,710
You want that to cancel out so that the model places

1351
00:53:40,710 --> 00:53:43,990
high probability exactly where the data lives.

1352
00:53:43,990 --> 00:53:48,373
So mathematically, here's how you derive that.

1353
00:53:48,373 --> 00:53:49,790
This is the kind of thing that you

1354
00:53:49,790 --> 00:53:52,910
might want to work through yourself after the lecture.

1355
00:53:52,910 --> 00:53:55,110
But I'll go through the steps for you here.

1356
00:53:55,110 --> 00:53:57,870
So we're going to do max likelihood learning

1357
00:53:57,870 --> 00:53:59,630
with energy-based models.

1358
00:53:59,630 --> 00:54:04,050
So max likelihood learning is, I want to update the parameters,

1359
00:54:04,050 --> 00:54:06,030
find the gradient of the parameters that

1360
00:54:06,030 --> 00:54:09,990
will increase the probability I place on the data.

1361
00:54:09,990 --> 00:54:13,220
And I'm writing it in terms of the expectation over samples,

1362
00:54:13,220 --> 00:54:17,480
but you would really do this on an empirical training set.

1363
00:54:17,480 --> 00:54:18,160
OK.

1364
00:54:18,160 --> 00:54:21,560
So I can rewrite my probability distribution

1365
00:54:21,560 --> 00:54:24,920
as an energy function divided by a normalization.

1366
00:54:24,920 --> 00:54:25,720
OK.

1367
00:54:25,720 --> 00:54:28,900
And now I'm parameterizing the energy function.

1368
00:54:28,900 --> 00:54:32,343
So the neural net E is just a mapping from data to a number.

1369
00:54:32,343 --> 00:54:33,760
And I'm going to parameterize that

1370
00:54:33,760 --> 00:54:37,520
by theta, which are the weights and biases

1371
00:54:37,520 --> 00:54:38,240
OK.

1372
00:54:38,240 --> 00:54:41,380
Now I'm going to take the log and split it into two terms.

1373
00:54:41,380 --> 00:54:47,160
It'll place high energy where the data lives, and then

1374
00:54:47,160 --> 00:54:52,040
have this extra term related to the normalization integral Z.

1375
00:54:52,040 --> 00:54:52,640
OK.

1376
00:54:52,640 --> 00:54:54,190
So that's called the positive term.

1377
00:54:54,190 --> 00:54:55,300
That's those green arrows.

1378
00:54:55,300 --> 00:54:56,120
And this is the negative term.

1379
00:54:56,120 --> 00:54:57,340
That was the red arrows.

1380
00:54:57,340 --> 00:54:58,820
So this one's easy, right?

1381
00:54:58,820 --> 00:55:03,460
This one is just, I have my samples from the data,

1382
00:55:03,460 --> 00:55:05,480
and I just do backprop to increase

1383
00:55:05,480 --> 00:55:07,168
the energy I place on those samples--

1384
00:55:07,168 --> 00:55:08,460
or, sorry, decrease the energy.

1385
00:55:08,460 --> 00:55:12,370
Remember, negative energy means high probability.

1386
00:55:12,370 --> 00:55:14,190
But how do you measure the normalization?

1387
00:55:14,190 --> 00:55:18,850
How do you measure the gradient of this normalization function?

1388
00:55:18,850 --> 00:55:20,890
So here's the full derivation, which

1389
00:55:20,890 --> 00:55:23,310
you might want to kind of walk through after the lecture.

1390
00:55:23,310 --> 00:55:24,730
But here's what it is.

1391
00:55:24,730 --> 00:55:28,690
It turns out that this thing has a very simple form.

1392
00:55:28,690 --> 00:55:34,670
So the gradient of this normalization function,

1393
00:55:34,670 --> 00:55:36,690
sometimes called the partition function,

1394
00:55:36,690 --> 00:55:40,930
is-- so grad of log of f of x is equal to 1

1395
00:55:40,930 --> 00:55:44,210
over f at x grad of f of x.

1396
00:55:44,210 --> 00:55:45,630
That's an identity from calculus.

1397
00:55:45,630 --> 00:55:47,610
It's a good thing to remember.

1398
00:55:47,610 --> 00:55:51,650
So this is equal to 1 over Z grad of Z.

1399
00:55:51,650 --> 00:55:54,650
Now I can write the definition of Z. The definition of Z

1400
00:55:54,650 --> 00:55:57,610
is the integral over x of--

1401
00:55:57,610 --> 00:55:59,345
this is like we're doing the softmax.

1402
00:55:59,345 --> 00:56:00,470
This is exactly what we do.

1403
00:56:00,470 --> 00:56:02,450
This is just the more general setting.

1404
00:56:02,450 --> 00:56:06,190
This is the normalizing constant,

1405
00:56:06,190 --> 00:56:09,810
so the integral over how much energy I place on all the data.

1406
00:56:09,810 --> 00:56:10,490
OK.

1407
00:56:10,490 --> 00:56:10,990
Oops.

1408
00:56:10,990 --> 00:56:12,130
Sorry.

1409
00:56:12,130 --> 00:56:18,990
So then, the next line is going to be chain rule.

1410
00:56:18,990 --> 00:56:20,850
So this is just the gradient of e

1411
00:56:20,850 --> 00:56:24,490
to the negative function of x, is-- here,

1412
00:56:24,490 --> 00:56:26,635
gradient of e to the x is e to the x times

1413
00:56:26,635 --> 00:56:28,510
the gradient of whatever was in the exponent.

1414
00:56:28,510 --> 00:56:30,450
That's just the chain rule.

1415
00:56:30,450 --> 00:56:34,970
And then this line here is just rearrangement of terms.

1416
00:56:34,970 --> 00:56:37,970
I'm taking this ratio, dividing by Z,

1417
00:56:37,970 --> 00:56:39,430
pulling it into the integral.

1418
00:56:39,430 --> 00:56:42,210
So now I get this term, which simplifies to p again.

1419
00:56:42,210 --> 00:56:43,710
So it's just rearrangement of terms.

1420
00:56:43,710 --> 00:56:45,752
That's the definition of p is equal to this term.

1421
00:56:45,752 --> 00:56:46,510
I found it again.

1422
00:56:46,510 --> 00:56:50,090
But now I have an integral of p of this thing.

1423
00:56:50,090 --> 00:56:52,450
And this is-- the whole point is to rearrange

1424
00:56:52,450 --> 00:56:55,150
the terms until I get something that looks an expectation.

1425
00:56:55,150 --> 00:56:56,110
That's an expectation.

1426
00:56:56,110 --> 00:56:59,390
An expectation is integral of p of x f of x.

1427
00:56:59,390 --> 00:57:03,550
That's expectation of f of x under the distribution p of x.

1428
00:57:03,550 --> 00:57:05,210
OK, so I got an expectation.

1429
00:57:05,210 --> 00:57:07,740
So this is all equal to expectation of samples

1430
00:57:07,740 --> 00:57:11,740
from the model of the gradient of the energy.

1431
00:57:11,740 --> 00:57:13,767
So what does that-- why did I do that?

1432
00:57:13,767 --> 00:57:16,100
Because if I get things into the form of an expectation,

1433
00:57:16,100 --> 00:57:18,960
I know how to approximate expectations on finite data.

1434
00:57:18,960 --> 00:57:20,500
I don't have to compute an integral

1435
00:57:20,500 --> 00:57:22,920
over some analytical infinite set.

1436
00:57:22,920 --> 00:57:24,620
I approximate expectation with samples,

1437
00:57:24,620 --> 00:57:26,040
and we know how to do that.

1438
00:57:26,040 --> 00:57:30,660
That's just sample from p, evaluate this, and backprop.

1439
00:57:30,660 --> 00:57:31,160
OK.

1440
00:57:31,160 --> 00:57:32,700
So here we go.

1441
00:57:32,700 --> 00:57:35,220
We converted this funny-looking term into now

1442
00:57:35,220 --> 00:57:36,980
just another expectation.

1443
00:57:36,980 --> 00:57:40,200
Both of these expectations can be approximated with samples.

1444
00:57:40,200 --> 00:57:42,580
This is samples from my training set.

1445
00:57:42,580 --> 00:57:45,540
And this is samples from my model.

1446
00:57:45,540 --> 00:57:47,780
And that's the gradient that will maximize

1447
00:57:47,780 --> 00:57:50,660
the likelihood of the density implied

1448
00:57:50,660 --> 00:57:55,260
by the energy for an energy-based model.

1449
00:57:55,260 --> 00:57:59,100
It's called contrastive divergence.

1450
00:57:59,100 --> 00:58:01,960
Any questions?

1451
00:58:01,960 --> 00:58:02,460
Yeah?

1452
00:58:02,460 --> 00:58:03,950
AUDIENCE: Just one question on the derivation.

1453
00:58:03,950 --> 00:58:05,410
You pulled out, like, a minus sign,

1454
00:58:05,410 --> 00:58:07,290
but it wasn't-- if I understand correctly,

1455
00:58:07,290 --> 00:58:08,332
it was not a subtraction.

1456
00:58:08,332 --> 00:58:09,450
It was times negative 1.

1457
00:58:09,450 --> 00:58:11,450
PHILLIP ISOLA: That-- I think-- oh, yeah, sorry.

1458
00:58:11,450 --> 00:58:13,602
That's a little-- this is a times negative 1.

1459
00:58:13,602 --> 00:58:14,810
Yeah, that's not subtraction.

1460
00:58:14,810 --> 00:58:17,630
That's probably confusing, yeah.

1461
00:58:17,630 --> 00:58:18,710
OK.

1462
00:58:18,710 --> 00:58:19,670
OK.

1463
00:58:19,670 --> 00:58:20,610
So that's the math.

1464
00:58:20,610 --> 00:58:24,090
But you can also just kind of keep in mind the intuition.

1465
00:58:24,090 --> 00:58:26,290
Push down energy where the data lives.

1466
00:58:26,290 --> 00:58:28,350
Push up energy where the model currently

1467
00:58:28,350 --> 00:58:31,428
places high probability.

1468
00:58:31,428 --> 00:58:33,970
Eventually, those cancel out, and the model has fit the data.

1469
00:58:36,750 --> 00:58:37,250
OK.

1470
00:58:37,250 --> 00:58:40,210
So we've seen a few different families of generative models

1471
00:58:40,210 --> 00:58:40,710
now.

1472
00:58:40,710 --> 00:58:43,150
We've seen that generative modeling takes data,

1473
00:58:43,150 --> 00:58:45,270
and you can learn a density function that assigns

1474
00:58:45,270 --> 00:58:46,650
a normalized score to the data.

1475
00:58:46,650 --> 00:58:48,310
You can define an energy function

1476
00:58:48,310 --> 00:58:53,710
that gives you a unnormalized score to the data.

1477
00:58:53,710 --> 00:58:58,430
And you can also directly sample by learning

1478
00:58:58,430 --> 00:59:02,920
a mapping from random noise or latent variables to data.

1479
00:59:02,920 --> 00:59:05,840
So you can represent the data generating

1480
00:59:05,840 --> 00:59:07,760
process in any of these ways, either directly

1481
00:59:07,760 --> 00:59:12,480
generate data from initial random variable or sample

1482
00:59:12,480 --> 00:59:15,680
from a density or energy fit to the data.

1483
00:59:15,680 --> 00:59:17,320
And each of these things, then, will

1484
00:59:17,320 --> 00:59:19,600
have a family of generative models

1485
00:59:19,600 --> 00:59:21,982
and deep generative models that have one of these forms.

1486
00:59:21,982 --> 00:59:23,440
And a lot of deep generative models

1487
00:59:23,440 --> 00:59:28,300
have multiple of these forms simultaneously.

1488
00:59:28,300 --> 00:59:32,400
So, finally, we're going to get to the popular, named,

1489
00:59:32,400 --> 00:59:33,460
deep generative models.

1490
00:59:33,460 --> 00:59:35,280
All of that was really just background

1491
00:59:35,280 --> 00:59:38,760
on generative modeling and probability theory.

1492
00:59:38,760 --> 00:59:40,220
So let's start.

1493
00:59:40,220 --> 00:59:41,720
We're going to talk about three now.

1494
00:59:41,720 --> 00:59:43,440
We're going to talk about autoregressive models, which

1495
00:59:43,440 --> 00:59:44,628
we've actually seen before.

1496
00:59:44,628 --> 00:59:46,420
We're going to talk about diffusion models.

1497
00:59:46,420 --> 00:59:48,545
And we're going to talk about GANs.

1498
00:59:48,545 --> 00:59:49,920
And then tomorrow-- not tomorrow,

1499
00:59:49,920 --> 00:59:54,300
but next week, we'll talk about VAEs, variational autoencoders.

1500
00:59:54,300 --> 00:59:57,200
So autoregressive models are kind of the simplest one

1501
00:59:57,200 --> 00:59:58,960
to understand, so we'll start there.

1502
00:59:58,960 --> 01:00:02,880
So autoregressive models are these next word predictors,

1503
01:00:02,880 --> 01:00:04,780
or next data point predictors.

1504
01:00:04,780 --> 01:00:08,140
So I do "once upon" and I try to predict what's the next word.

1505
01:00:08,140 --> 01:00:09,700
"A."

1506
01:00:09,700 --> 01:00:11,068
And then I repeat the process.

1507
01:00:11,068 --> 01:00:12,360
So we've seen this a few times.

1508
01:00:12,360 --> 01:00:14,320
"Time."

1509
01:00:14,320 --> 01:00:16,020
So how is this a generative model?

1510
01:00:16,020 --> 01:00:19,600
What probability distribution is it sampling from?

1511
01:00:19,600 --> 01:00:22,180
And I don't have to go in temporal order.

1512
01:00:22,180 --> 01:00:24,880
That's just a particular choice.

1513
01:00:24,880 --> 01:00:26,238
So, recap.

1514
01:00:26,238 --> 01:00:27,780
I think I've shown this slide before.

1515
01:00:27,780 --> 01:00:29,320
We trained an autoregressive model

1516
01:00:29,320 --> 01:00:31,180
with standard supervised learning.

1517
01:00:31,180 --> 01:00:32,940
We have a data set of sentences.

1518
01:00:32,940 --> 01:00:37,920
We just chop the sentence into the prefix and the final word,

1519
01:00:37,920 --> 01:00:41,240
or the next word at some point in the sentence.

1520
01:00:41,240 --> 01:00:42,860
That's a supervised learning problem.

1521
01:00:42,860 --> 01:00:45,100
I can just try to treat it as classification.

1522
01:00:45,100 --> 01:00:46,760
Predict the next word

1523
01:00:46,760 --> 01:00:47,260
OK.

1524
01:00:47,260 --> 01:00:48,593
So that's my learning algorithm.

1525
01:00:48,593 --> 01:00:51,320
And my sampling algorithm-- now I'm going to call it sampling--

1526
01:00:51,320 --> 01:00:52,960
is I run--

1527
01:00:52,960 --> 01:00:55,640
I predict the next word, but I'm not

1528
01:00:55,640 --> 01:00:58,870
going to necessarily just predict one word.

1529
01:00:58,870 --> 01:01:00,510
I'll predict a distribution over words.

1530
01:01:00,510 --> 01:01:03,003
So it's like, if each word is treated as a class,

1531
01:01:03,003 --> 01:01:04,670
I can output a categorical distribution.

1532
01:01:04,670 --> 01:01:07,770
I can do softmax regression and sample from that distribution.

1533
01:01:10,476 --> 01:01:11,170
OK.

1534
01:01:11,170 --> 01:01:14,230
So why is this a valid probability model?

1535
01:01:14,230 --> 01:01:17,090
Why does this actually do max likelihood density

1536
01:01:17,090 --> 01:01:19,410
modeling of a distribution?

1537
01:01:19,410 --> 01:01:22,170
The reason is because of this equation, which is, I think,

1538
01:01:22,170 --> 01:01:24,230
sometimes called the chain rule of probability.

1539
01:01:24,230 --> 01:01:30,350
But it says that the probability a set of words-- or a vector X,

1540
01:01:30,350 --> 01:01:33,810
so a set of data points X--

1541
01:01:33,810 --> 01:01:37,750
is equal to the probability of x2 given x1.

1542
01:01:37,750 --> 01:01:40,290
So x1 through xn are the elements of my vector

1543
01:01:40,290 --> 01:01:44,010
X. So I just do probability of x1 times probability of x2 times

1544
01:01:44,010 --> 01:01:44,670
x1.

1545
01:01:44,670 --> 01:01:47,770
So that becomes probability of x1 and x2.

1546
01:01:47,770 --> 01:01:50,130
And then I multiply that by probability of x3, given x1

1547
01:01:50,130 --> 01:01:50,810
and x2.

1548
01:01:50,810 --> 01:01:51,310
OK.

1549
01:01:51,310 --> 01:01:55,330
So this is an identity, an identity from probability.

1550
01:01:55,330 --> 01:01:56,140
OK.

1551
01:01:56,140 --> 01:01:58,820
So I can just write that in the shorthand form

1552
01:01:58,820 --> 01:02:01,818
as this is product over all the conditionals of the next token

1553
01:02:01,818 --> 01:02:03,860
given the previous tokens, or the next word given

1554
01:02:03,860 --> 01:02:05,693
the previous word, the next data point given

1555
01:02:05,693 --> 01:02:07,650
previous data points.

1556
01:02:07,650 --> 01:02:09,900
OK, so here's what that looks like for the probability

1557
01:02:09,900 --> 01:02:12,140
of "once upon a time" is equal to the probability of "once"

1558
01:02:12,140 --> 01:02:13,973
times the probability of "upon" given "once"

1559
01:02:13,973 --> 01:02:15,340
times the probability of--

1560
01:02:15,340 --> 01:02:15,960
right.

1561
01:02:15,960 --> 01:02:16,580
OK.

1562
01:02:16,580 --> 01:02:19,820
So it's the product of all those conditional probabilities.

1563
01:02:19,820 --> 01:02:22,340
And autoregressive modeling is simply

1564
01:02:22,340 --> 01:02:25,700
modeling all of those conditional probabilities

1565
01:02:25,700 --> 01:02:29,160
in a sequence and trying to maximize the likelihood.

1566
01:02:29,160 --> 01:02:31,660
So how do I model probability of "time" given "once upon a"?

1567
01:02:31,660 --> 01:02:34,880
I just do next word classifier.

1568
01:02:34,880 --> 01:02:37,100
So I just output a categorical distribution

1569
01:02:37,100 --> 01:02:39,077
over my vocabulary size.

1570
01:02:39,077 --> 01:02:40,660
The number of categories is the number

1571
01:02:40,660 --> 01:02:42,320
of words I could possibly predict.

1572
01:02:42,320 --> 01:02:44,860
So "once upon a," I output a probability mass function,

1573
01:02:44,860 --> 01:02:48,233
a categorical distribution, over all the possible next words.

1574
01:02:48,233 --> 01:02:49,900
There's a few other ways you could do it

1575
01:02:49,900 --> 01:02:51,400
in natural language processing.

1576
01:02:51,400 --> 01:02:54,660
But this is kind of the basic idea.

1577
01:02:54,660 --> 01:02:56,140
OK.

1578
01:02:56,140 --> 01:02:57,520
What about in image processing?

1579
01:02:57,520 --> 01:02:59,437
Here's how you would do it in image processing

1580
01:02:59,437 --> 01:03:00,860
for an autoregressive model.

1581
01:03:00,860 --> 01:03:03,580
I am going to start drawing this bird,

1582
01:03:03,580 --> 01:03:05,820
and at any point in my drawing, I'm

1583
01:03:05,820 --> 01:03:08,260
going to predict the color of the next pixel given

1584
01:03:08,260 --> 01:03:10,300
all the previous pixels.

1585
01:03:10,300 --> 01:03:10,800
OK.

1586
01:03:10,800 --> 01:03:14,495
So I can quantize my pixels--

1587
01:03:14,495 --> 01:03:17,120
just like I showed in one of the colorization examples earlier,

1588
01:03:17,120 --> 01:03:19,260
I can quantize my pixels into classes--

1589
01:03:19,260 --> 01:03:21,460
red, green, blue, and so forth-- and output

1590
01:03:21,460 --> 01:03:23,480
a categorical distribution over the classes.

1591
01:03:23,480 --> 01:03:26,020
That's just softmax regression, that we've seen before.

1592
01:03:26,020 --> 01:03:27,420
And then I sample from it by just

1593
01:03:27,420 --> 01:03:29,160
sampling from a categorical distribution.

1594
01:03:29,160 --> 01:03:30,422
So if I have--

1595
01:03:30,422 --> 01:03:32,880
it's easy to sample from a categorical distribution, right?

1596
01:03:32,880 --> 01:03:36,060
I can just take the unit interval 0 to 1,

1597
01:03:36,060 --> 01:03:38,960
I can take a uniform sample from 0 to 1,

1598
01:03:38,960 --> 01:03:41,860
and I can walk through my probability vector

1599
01:03:41,860 --> 01:03:44,940
until I get to--

1600
01:03:44,940 --> 01:03:48,820
until I get to the segment that has been sampled from.

1601
01:03:48,820 --> 01:03:52,070
So we can easily sample from a categorical distribution.

1602
01:03:52,070 --> 01:03:54,290
And then we just repeat the process.

1603
01:03:54,290 --> 01:03:56,970
So I go from nothing to next pixel, next pixel, next pixel.

1604
01:03:56,970 --> 01:04:02,110
It's the same as predicting the next word in a sentence.

1605
01:04:02,110 --> 01:04:04,040
OK.

1606
01:04:04,040 --> 01:04:05,790
And this is just, again--

1607
01:04:05,790 --> 01:04:07,710
I think one of the early slides in this course

1608
01:04:07,710 --> 01:04:10,810
was classifying images into categories,

1609
01:04:10,810 --> 01:04:13,170
and here, I'm classifying next pixel into categories.

1610
01:04:13,170 --> 01:04:18,190
And my loss function is the likelihood that my model--

1611
01:04:18,190 --> 01:04:20,810
my model, which is not really specified here,

1612
01:04:20,810 --> 01:04:24,588
but anyway-- f theta places on the ground-truth class.

1613
01:04:24,588 --> 01:04:26,630
This is the amount of probability my model places

1614
01:04:26,630 --> 01:04:28,610
on the ground-truth class.

1615
01:04:28,610 --> 01:04:33,550
I want to maximize that, max likelihood modeling.

1616
01:04:33,550 --> 01:04:34,270
OK.

1617
01:04:34,270 --> 01:04:37,750
So yeah, just spelling it out for a different data type.

1618
01:04:37,750 --> 01:04:39,790
So now my supervised data I learned from

1619
01:04:39,790 --> 01:04:43,710
is examples of patches of pixels and the next pixel

1620
01:04:43,710 --> 01:04:45,030
in the sequence.

1621
01:04:45,030 --> 01:04:47,450
I do supervised softmax regression,

1622
01:04:47,450 --> 01:04:49,830
and I can predict the next pixel in the sequence given

1623
01:04:49,830 --> 01:04:51,800
the previous pixels, and I can actually

1624
01:04:51,800 --> 01:04:55,260
sample to create a randomized variety of birds in this way.

1625
01:04:58,220 --> 01:04:58,720
Yeah?

1626
01:04:58,720 --> 01:04:59,960
Question.

1627
01:04:59,960 --> 01:05:01,800
AUDIENCE: So would you characterize

1628
01:05:01,800 --> 01:05:03,680
this as a density model?

1629
01:05:03,680 --> 01:05:06,200
Can you draw that correspondence lower?

1630
01:05:06,200 --> 01:05:08,617
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so what would I characterize this as?

1631
01:05:08,617 --> 01:05:11,980
A density model, sampler, energy model?

1632
01:05:11,980 --> 01:05:13,360
What do you all think?

1633
01:05:13,360 --> 01:05:14,500
Who thinks density model?

1634
01:05:14,500 --> 01:05:15,620
Raise your hand.

1635
01:05:15,620 --> 01:05:16,120
OK.

1636
01:05:16,120 --> 01:05:18,040
Who thinks energy model?

1637
01:05:18,040 --> 01:05:23,840
Who thinks sampler or the direct approach?

1638
01:05:23,840 --> 01:05:27,000
So I think density model was the highest vote.

1639
01:05:27,000 --> 01:05:29,340
I think that these things are a little fuzzy.

1640
01:05:29,340 --> 01:05:32,060
You can actually-- I'm not sure there's a correct answer here.

1641
01:05:32,060 --> 01:05:34,760
But I would characterize it as a density model

1642
01:05:34,760 --> 01:05:39,520
because we're outputting the density over the next pixel

1643
01:05:39,520 --> 01:05:41,120
given the previous pixels.

1644
01:05:41,120 --> 01:05:42,720
And we're actually going to do that

1645
01:05:42,720 --> 01:05:44,880
for every pixel in a sequence or every word

1646
01:05:44,880 --> 01:05:47,600
in a sequence to get a probability that's explicitly

1647
01:05:47,600 --> 01:05:48,260
represented.

1648
01:05:48,260 --> 01:05:50,290
So this is actually-- you can multiply

1649
01:05:50,290 --> 01:05:52,745
all of those probabilities together,

1650
01:05:52,745 --> 01:05:54,870
and you'll get the probability of the entire image.

1651
01:05:54,870 --> 01:05:56,970
So the product of all the conditional probabilities

1652
01:05:56,970 --> 01:05:58,595
is the probability of the entire image.

1653
01:05:58,595 --> 01:06:01,050
That's a density that integrates to 1.

1654
01:06:01,050 --> 01:06:03,690
So I would call it a density model.

1655
01:06:03,690 --> 01:06:07,062
OK.

1656
01:06:07,062 --> 01:06:09,970
And people do this in a lot of sequential data modeling

1657
01:06:09,970 --> 01:06:10,550
problems.

1658
01:06:10,550 --> 01:06:13,387
Time series modeling is often autoregressive.

1659
01:06:13,387 --> 01:06:14,970
So here's another example, where we're

1660
01:06:14,970 --> 01:06:18,910
going to predict the next sound wave in a sound.

1661
01:06:18,910 --> 01:06:22,330
So this is, like, for music modeling or audio modeling.

1662
01:06:22,330 --> 01:06:24,450
I simply predict the next--

1663
01:06:27,970 --> 01:06:29,470
I have some representation of sound,

1664
01:06:29,470 --> 01:06:32,930
and I predict what the sound will be on the next time step.

1665
01:06:32,930 --> 01:06:35,530
So it looks exactly like language modeling.

1666
01:06:35,530 --> 01:06:39,530
This is just autoregressive modeling on another domain.

1667
01:06:39,530 --> 01:06:41,570
OK.

1668
01:06:41,570 --> 01:06:42,450
Right.

1669
01:06:42,450 --> 01:06:47,050
So maybe it was a little-- let me-- sorry.

1670
01:06:47,050 --> 01:06:50,170
Let me just go up here.

1671
01:06:50,170 --> 01:06:56,330
So just to clarify, I'm trying to maximize the likelihood

1672
01:06:56,330 --> 01:06:57,855
I place on my training data.

1673
01:06:57,855 --> 01:06:59,730
So the likelihood I place on my training data

1674
01:06:59,730 --> 01:07:01,147
is the product over the likelihood

1675
01:07:01,147 --> 01:07:06,930
I place on each next observation in this sequence.

1676
01:07:06,930 --> 01:07:09,810
And if I'm using log probabilities,

1677
01:07:09,810 --> 01:07:12,770
it becomes the sum over the log probabilities.

1678
01:07:12,770 --> 01:07:16,490
So the likelihood that the autoregressive model

1679
01:07:16,490 --> 01:07:21,810
places on a training example is the sum of the log probabilities

1680
01:07:21,810 --> 01:07:24,570
it places on every next pixel in the sequence.

1681
01:07:24,570 --> 01:07:27,690
And I just sum all those up and backprop.

1682
01:07:27,690 --> 01:07:29,450
And that's how you do optimization.

1683
01:07:29,450 --> 01:07:34,850
So that's kind of what this picture is showing,

1684
01:07:34,850 --> 01:07:36,242
is I will just make a prediction,

1685
01:07:36,242 --> 01:07:37,950
then I'll compare it to the ground truth,

1686
01:07:37,950 --> 01:07:41,210
measure the probability under my categorical distribution.

1687
01:07:41,210 --> 01:07:43,355
Then I have output of that ground-truth point,

1688
01:07:43,355 --> 01:07:44,980
and I'll sum up all those probabilities

1689
01:07:44,980 --> 01:07:46,360
and backprop through this graph.

1690
01:07:46,360 --> 01:07:48,152
So that's how you actually train the thing.

1691
01:07:50,580 --> 01:07:53,260
OK.

1692
01:07:53,260 --> 01:07:53,760
OK.

1693
01:07:53,760 --> 01:07:56,200
So same as what we saw in other sequence modeling

1694
01:07:56,200 --> 01:07:57,560
in previous lectures, like RNNs.

1695
01:07:57,560 --> 01:07:59,243
We talked about, you output predictions,

1696
01:07:59,243 --> 01:08:00,160
you measure the error.

1697
01:08:00,160 --> 01:08:03,820
But now I'm just giving it this probabilistic language.

1698
01:08:03,820 --> 01:08:06,740
So next model that we're going to talk about

1699
01:08:06,740 --> 01:08:09,580
is diffusion model.

1700
01:08:09,580 --> 01:08:13,628
So diffusion models are really clever because they

1701
01:08:13,628 --> 01:08:14,920
make the following observation.

1702
01:08:14,920 --> 01:08:18,260
They say that-- you know, it's pretty hard to--

1703
01:08:18,260 --> 01:08:21,240
these are one of these more direct generative models.

1704
01:08:21,240 --> 01:08:25,220
So they're directly mapping from a random dice roll to a sample,

1705
01:08:25,220 --> 01:08:30,380
as opposed to going through a density or an energy function.

1706
01:08:30,380 --> 01:08:33,580
So diffusion models make the very clever observation

1707
01:08:33,580 --> 01:08:38,300
that it's very hard to convert noise, random variables,

1708
01:08:38,300 --> 01:08:42,020
into data, structured objects, but it's really easy

1709
01:08:42,020 --> 01:08:44,250
to convert structured objects into noise,

1710
01:08:44,250 --> 01:08:47,069
into random variables with simple distributions.

1711
01:08:47,069 --> 01:08:50,550
So just imagine that these birds are like balloon animals filled

1712
01:08:50,550 --> 01:08:54,330
with some kind of colored gas, and then you pop the balloon.

1713
01:08:54,330 --> 01:08:55,050
What happens?

1714
01:08:55,050 --> 01:08:57,399
All the particles spread out randomly,

1715
01:08:57,399 --> 01:08:59,149
and eventually, they become just uniformly

1716
01:08:59,149 --> 01:09:01,470
distributed in a space.

1717
01:09:01,470 --> 01:09:03,569
Physically, that's called a diffusion process.

1718
01:09:03,569 --> 01:09:05,830
And diffusion models are exactly the same thing,

1719
01:09:05,830 --> 01:09:09,210
except you're diffusing in some high-dimensional data space.

1720
01:09:09,210 --> 01:09:12,350
Whatever your data is, it might be a tensor,

1721
01:09:12,350 --> 01:09:15,310
and you're diffusing by just randomly perturbing it

1722
01:09:15,310 --> 01:09:19,270
until it becomes uniform, or it has a simple distribution,

1723
01:09:19,270 --> 01:09:20,990
typically Gaussian.

1724
01:09:20,990 --> 01:09:23,529
So converting images to noise is really easy.

1725
01:09:23,529 --> 01:09:24,870
Here's how it looks like.

1726
01:09:24,870 --> 01:09:29,010
I just take an image and I let the pixels diffuse outward.

1727
01:09:29,010 --> 01:09:32,158
So I simply take every pixel and I add a little bit of noise.

1728
01:09:32,158 --> 01:09:34,450
And I add a little more noise, add a little more noise.

1729
01:09:34,450 --> 01:09:35,867
Eventually, I get to a state which

1730
01:09:35,867 --> 01:09:38,689
is, like, fully entropic, a very simple distribution.

1731
01:09:38,689 --> 01:09:39,732
Maybe it's uniform.

1732
01:09:39,732 --> 01:09:41,149
In the models that we'll see, it's

1733
01:09:41,149 --> 01:09:42,399
actually going to be Gaussian.

1734
01:09:42,399 --> 01:09:44,850
If I just add a sequence of Gaussian random perturbations,

1735
01:09:44,850 --> 01:09:50,870
I end up with a unit Gaussian at the very end of that sequence.

1736
01:09:50,870 --> 01:09:54,470
So diffusion models convert data to noise

1737
01:09:54,470 --> 01:09:57,470
and then just learn to reverse the process.

1738
01:09:57,470 --> 01:09:59,030
So easy, right?

1739
01:09:59,030 --> 01:10:00,510
So it's extremely hard to know how

1740
01:10:00,510 --> 01:10:02,650
to do that mapping from noise to data,

1741
01:10:02,650 --> 01:10:06,590
but I supervise it by telling it the exact path to take.

1742
01:10:06,590 --> 01:10:08,190
So every little step of denoising,

1743
01:10:08,190 --> 01:10:11,582
of going from noise to a little less noise, is a very simple--

1744
01:10:11,582 --> 01:10:13,290
just remove a little bit of noise, right?

1745
01:10:13,290 --> 01:10:14,530
It's a very easy thing to model.

1746
01:10:14,530 --> 01:10:16,197
I can model it with supervised learning.

1747
01:10:16,197 --> 01:10:19,790
So I simply-- I've created a lot of noisy sequences

1748
01:10:19,790 --> 01:10:22,590
like this on my training data.

1749
01:10:22,590 --> 01:10:25,230
I turn them all around, and I then chop it up.

1750
01:10:25,230 --> 01:10:28,330
And for every noisy to less noisy pair,

1751
01:10:28,330 --> 01:10:31,850
that's like an xy target, a noisy to a less noisy.

1752
01:10:31,850 --> 01:10:35,510
And we'll index the noise level with t, so higher noise

1753
01:10:35,510 --> 01:10:37,590
means higher t.

1754
01:10:37,590 --> 01:10:40,540
I'll just use supervised learning to reverse the process,

1755
01:10:40,540 --> 01:10:46,080
and this learns a mapping from random noise to images.

1756
01:10:46,080 --> 01:10:50,172
And to sample an image, I simply can sample a random noise vector

1757
01:10:50,172 --> 01:10:51,880
and put it through that mapping that I've

1758
01:10:51,880 --> 01:10:54,910
learned, that reverse diffusion process, as it's known.

1759
01:10:54,910 --> 01:10:56,660
OK, so here, again, is what it looks like.

1760
01:10:56,660 --> 01:11:00,000
I create a data set of noisy and less noisy images

1761
01:11:00,000 --> 01:11:03,560
by having started with less noisy images and adding noise.

1762
01:11:03,560 --> 01:11:05,500
And I do supervised learning on that.

1763
01:11:05,500 --> 01:11:08,900
That could be as simple as just, like, mean squared regression,

1764
01:11:08,900 --> 01:11:12,620
try to predict, according to L, which might be the squared loss,

1765
01:11:12,620 --> 01:11:14,620
the less noisy image from the more noisy image.

1766
01:11:14,620 --> 01:11:16,828
And you could do this with any data, not just images.

1767
01:11:16,828 --> 01:11:18,120
It's just an example.

1768
01:11:18,120 --> 01:11:19,440
OK.

1769
01:11:19,440 --> 01:11:21,800
And this converts generative modeling

1770
01:11:21,800 --> 01:11:26,280
into a bunch of supervised, tiny little prediction problems.

1771
01:11:26,280 --> 01:11:26,780
OK.

1772
01:11:26,780 --> 01:11:28,530
I'll get to the question in just a second.

1773
01:11:31,080 --> 01:11:32,440
So-- yeah.

1774
01:11:32,440 --> 01:11:32,940
OK.

1775
01:11:32,940 --> 01:11:33,980
Actually, I'll do the question now.

1776
01:11:33,980 --> 01:11:34,480
Yeah.

1777
01:11:34,480 --> 01:11:35,080
Go ahead.

1778
01:11:35,080 --> 01:11:37,330
AUDIENCE: I wanted to ask, so in each step,

1779
01:11:37,330 --> 01:11:41,663
is each step the same model?

1780
01:11:41,663 --> 01:11:43,330
Or are there a bunch of different models

1781
01:11:43,330 --> 01:11:45,010
doing each step individually?

1782
01:11:45,010 --> 01:11:47,170
PHILLIP ISOLA: So is each step the same model?

1783
01:11:47,170 --> 01:11:51,270
Is f the same function for all t, or is it different for t?

1784
01:11:51,270 --> 01:11:52,930
Either option is fine.

1785
01:11:52,930 --> 01:11:55,250
The most common option is you make f a function

1786
01:11:55,250 --> 01:11:57,930
of x, and you condition on t.

1787
01:11:57,930 --> 01:12:01,490
So you just tell the network what timestep you're on.

1788
01:12:01,490 --> 01:12:03,950
But that's a modeling choice.

1789
01:12:03,950 --> 01:12:05,950
It's not fundamental.

1790
01:12:05,950 --> 01:12:06,450
OK.

1791
01:12:06,450 --> 01:12:08,130
So if I--

1792
01:12:08,130 --> 01:12:10,730
I'll get to that equation in a minute.

1793
01:12:10,730 --> 01:12:13,010
But anyway, if I want to sample a new image,

1794
01:12:13,010 --> 01:12:15,530
well, I've converted all of my data at the end,

1795
01:12:15,530 --> 01:12:17,290
at t equals big T-- meaning, like,

1796
01:12:17,290 --> 01:12:19,030
as t goes to infinity, it turns--

1797
01:12:19,030 --> 01:12:21,690
if I'm adding Gaussian noise, it asymptotically

1798
01:12:21,690 --> 01:12:26,650
becomes a unit Gaussian, if I do it with the right scaling.

1799
01:12:26,650 --> 01:12:29,490
And that means that I can now, if I reverse the process,

1800
01:12:29,490 --> 01:12:31,110
start with a unit Gaussian sample,

1801
01:12:31,110 --> 01:12:33,410
roll the dice, which are unit Gaussian variables,

1802
01:12:33,410 --> 01:12:35,380
and then I denoise with my learned function.

1803
01:12:35,380 --> 01:12:38,540
And every unit Gaussian sample gives me a different noisy

1804
01:12:38,540 --> 01:12:41,237
image, which are like the latent variables that

1805
01:12:41,237 --> 01:12:42,820
specify all the properties of the data

1806
01:12:42,820 --> 01:12:45,740
that you will generate in the end.

1807
01:12:45,740 --> 01:12:48,220
So sampling is really easy.

1808
01:12:48,220 --> 01:12:51,020
It's just slow because you have to do this, run

1809
01:12:51,020 --> 01:12:53,060
the denoising neural network, over and over

1810
01:12:53,060 --> 01:12:55,180
and over again, like, for t time steps.

1811
01:12:55,180 --> 01:12:58,933
And when these first came out, t was large, like, a thousand.

1812
01:12:58,933 --> 01:13:01,600
Now people are showing how to do this with t being pretty small,

1813
01:13:01,600 --> 01:13:03,820
like, even going down to 1.

1814
01:13:03,820 --> 01:13:06,520
But you typically have to do a few time steps.

1815
01:13:06,520 --> 01:13:07,020
OK.

1816
01:13:07,020 --> 01:13:11,140
So here's the Gaussian diffusion model mathematically.

1817
01:13:11,140 --> 01:13:14,555
So we're going to define a data generating process that

1818
01:13:14,555 --> 01:13:16,180
starts from a simple distribution, unit

1819
01:13:16,180 --> 01:13:18,660
Gaussian, and maps to data.

1820
01:13:18,660 --> 01:13:23,100
And we're going to train it by training the denoiser that

1821
01:13:23,100 --> 01:13:26,540
predicts the less noisy image from the more noisy image,

1822
01:13:26,540 --> 01:13:30,020
to invert the noiser, which is a simple deterministic process

1823
01:13:30,020 --> 01:13:34,020
that adds Gaussian noise to an image.

1824
01:13:34,020 --> 01:13:36,600
And we can condition the denoiser on the timestep

1825
01:13:36,600 --> 01:13:39,240
so it knows, What is the current noise level?

1826
01:13:39,240 --> 01:13:42,940
And that helps it be a better predictor of what the true data

1827
01:13:42,940 --> 01:13:45,020
point should have been.

1828
01:13:45,020 --> 01:13:49,540
So the forward process is simply add

1829
01:13:49,540 --> 01:13:51,820
a Gaussian noise, which is a unit Gaussian,

1830
01:13:51,820 --> 01:13:54,860
epsilon t, and scale that noise according

1831
01:13:54,860 --> 01:13:59,800
to some scale beta, which can be time-varying as well.

1832
01:13:59,800 --> 01:14:01,300
And there's some engineering details

1833
01:14:01,300 --> 01:14:03,860
about how you select beta.

1834
01:14:03,860 --> 01:14:05,940
But in general, you can do this in such a way

1835
01:14:05,940 --> 01:14:08,980
that at the end of the day, you will get unit Gaussian

1836
01:14:08,980 --> 01:14:13,240
noise, asymptotically, as t goes to infinity.

1837
01:14:13,240 --> 01:14:15,003
OK, the reverse process.

1838
01:14:15,003 --> 01:14:16,420
In fact, with the reverse process,

1839
01:14:16,420 --> 01:14:20,900
you're going to treat every little step as a small Gaussian

1840
01:14:20,900 --> 01:14:23,540
max likelihood density modeling problem.

1841
01:14:23,540 --> 01:14:25,460
So the reverse process, f is not going

1842
01:14:25,460 --> 01:14:27,555
to just predict the noiseless image--

1843
01:14:27,555 --> 01:14:29,680
or, not going to just predict the less noisy image.

1844
01:14:29,680 --> 01:14:33,270
It's going to predict the mean of a Gaussian distribution

1845
01:14:33,270 --> 01:14:35,950
over the less noisy image.

1846
01:14:35,950 --> 01:14:38,050
This is just-- there's various reasons,

1847
01:14:38,050 --> 01:14:41,150
but this gives kind of a nice, mathematical, probabilistic

1848
01:14:41,150 --> 01:14:43,710
interpretation to what's going on.

1849
01:14:43,710 --> 01:14:46,750
OK, so the next denoised timestep

1850
01:14:46,750 --> 01:14:48,990
will be a sample from a Gaussian whose mean

1851
01:14:48,990 --> 01:14:51,050
is the output of my neural network denoiser.

1852
01:14:54,890 --> 01:14:57,990
So people like to use these terms,

1853
01:14:57,990 --> 01:15:01,910
p for one of the processes and q for the reverse process.

1854
01:15:01,910 --> 01:15:04,950
And it connects to the standard notation and things

1855
01:15:04,950 --> 01:15:06,530
like called variational inference.

1856
01:15:06,530 --> 01:15:08,447
But you don't have to worry about it too much.

1857
01:15:08,447 --> 01:15:11,990
We'll just say the forward process is q, which, x of t

1858
01:15:11,990 --> 01:15:15,650
is a Gaussian centered on x of t minus 1.

1859
01:15:15,650 --> 01:15:17,790
And the reverse process I'm trying to learn

1860
01:15:17,790 --> 01:15:21,190
is another Gaussian, but it's centered on a function

1861
01:15:21,190 --> 01:15:22,790
of the noisy image.

1862
01:15:22,790 --> 01:15:23,290
OK.

1863
01:15:23,290 --> 01:15:26,190
So the forward process is just add noise,

1864
01:15:26,190 --> 01:15:27,790
and the reverse process is predict

1865
01:15:27,790 --> 01:15:31,280
the mean of a reverse Gaussian, so

1866
01:15:31,280 --> 01:15:35,400
predict the mean and then sample from that Gaussian.

1867
01:15:35,400 --> 01:15:36,530
OK.

1868
01:15:36,530 --> 01:15:38,280
There's a lot of variations and other ways

1869
01:15:38,280 --> 01:15:40,280
you could do this, this kind of vanilla Gaussian

1870
01:15:40,280 --> 01:15:43,280
diffusion model.

1871
01:15:43,280 --> 01:15:47,440
And if you want, I wrote a very vanilla version of this.

1872
01:15:47,440 --> 01:15:50,000
So you can play with the Colab if you want.

1873
01:15:50,000 --> 01:15:54,000
But now, I think there's probably better ones out there.

1874
01:15:54,000 --> 01:15:56,960
I want to make the connection between diffusion models

1875
01:15:56,960 --> 01:15:59,000
and autoregressive models.

1876
01:15:59,000 --> 01:16:00,920
So they're almost the same thing.

1877
01:16:00,920 --> 01:16:04,080
So both of them use a very simple trick.

1878
01:16:04,080 --> 01:16:07,040
You change generative modeling of a high-dimensional structured

1879
01:16:07,040 --> 01:16:11,680
object into a sequence of very, very simple modeling problems.

1880
01:16:11,680 --> 01:16:16,200
In autoregressive modeling, you convert your data

1881
01:16:16,200 --> 01:16:20,360
into a sequence of simple categorical density modeling

1882
01:16:20,360 --> 01:16:22,860
problems, a.k.a. classification problems.

1883
01:16:25,800 --> 01:16:26,300
OK.

1884
01:16:26,300 --> 01:16:29,740
Again, you're free to choose other types of simple sequences,

1885
01:16:29,740 --> 01:16:31,680
but that's just the most common choice.

1886
01:16:31,680 --> 01:16:32,700
So think of it this way.

1887
01:16:32,700 --> 01:16:35,260
You start with a structured data object,

1888
01:16:35,260 --> 01:16:39,340
and you remove one data point at a time until you get to nothing,

1889
01:16:39,340 --> 01:16:42,065
and then that supervises the reverse process.

1890
01:16:42,065 --> 01:16:44,440
Diffusion model, you start with a structured, complicated

1891
01:16:44,440 --> 01:16:44,980
object.

1892
01:16:44,980 --> 01:16:48,920
You add noise kind of globally, to every data point, until you

1893
01:16:48,920 --> 01:16:52,120
get to a very simple distribution you

1894
01:16:52,120 --> 01:16:54,480
can sample from, and then you supervise

1895
01:16:54,480 --> 01:16:55,585
it to reverse the process.

1896
01:16:55,585 --> 01:16:56,960
This is your training data that's

1897
01:16:56,960 --> 01:16:58,585
going to supervise the reverse process.

1898
01:16:58,585 --> 01:17:00,085
This is your training data that will

1899
01:17:00,085 --> 01:17:01,413
supervise the reverse process.

1900
01:17:01,413 --> 01:17:02,580
This is easy to sample from.

1901
01:17:02,580 --> 01:17:04,780
You're just sampling one pixel color.

1902
01:17:04,780 --> 01:17:05,600
OK.

1903
01:17:05,600 --> 01:17:07,840
Let's say I have 256 possible colors.

1904
01:17:07,840 --> 01:17:09,800
I just take a random--

1905
01:17:09,800 --> 01:17:12,840
I have a categorical distribution

1906
01:17:12,840 --> 01:17:16,580
over the 256 possible colors that occur in natural images.

1907
01:17:16,580 --> 01:17:17,480
Super easy.

1908
01:17:17,480 --> 01:17:20,160
And then I just reverse the process

1909
01:17:20,160 --> 01:17:24,120
by trying to classify the next pixel given the previous pixel.

1910
01:17:24,120 --> 01:17:27,050
So the whole point is that a common strategy

1911
01:17:27,050 --> 01:17:31,130
in generative modeling is turn a complicated structure

1912
01:17:31,130 --> 01:17:34,910
of distribution into a sequence of simple distributions,

1913
01:17:34,910 --> 01:17:37,150
and model that sequence of simple distributions.

1914
01:17:40,610 --> 01:17:43,410
OK.

1915
01:17:43,410 --> 01:17:43,910
OK.

1916
01:17:43,910 --> 01:17:46,157
So in the last two minutes, I'll just do GANs.

1917
01:17:46,157 --> 01:17:46,990
They're very simple.

1918
01:17:46,990 --> 01:17:48,130
Don't worry.

1919
01:17:48,130 --> 01:17:50,853
I know there's a lot in this lecture, but I'm hoping that--

1920
01:17:50,853 --> 01:17:52,270
I want to give you the intuitions,

1921
01:17:52,270 --> 01:17:54,665
and then in the problem sets and in the readings,

1922
01:17:54,665 --> 01:17:56,290
you can dig a little more into the math

1923
01:17:56,290 --> 01:17:57,570
and you can work it out.

1924
01:17:57,570 --> 01:18:01,650
So GANs are a whole other class of generative model.

1925
01:18:01,650 --> 01:18:06,005
GANs are a-- OK.

1926
01:18:06,005 --> 01:18:08,130
I'll tell you what they are, and then I'll ask you,

1927
01:18:08,130 --> 01:18:09,390
Are they direct or indirect?

1928
01:18:09,390 --> 01:18:11,490
So they start with a latent variable, which

1929
01:18:11,490 --> 01:18:15,770
is a vector of random numbers, and they map it

1930
01:18:15,770 --> 01:18:18,370
through a generator network to an image.

1931
01:18:18,370 --> 01:18:20,050
And then they play this game where

1932
01:18:20,050 --> 01:18:23,220
they ask another neural network to decide if the image looks

1933
01:18:23,220 --> 01:18:26,700
like a real data point or a synthetic data point sampled

1934
01:18:26,700 --> 01:18:27,580
from the model.

1935
01:18:27,580 --> 01:18:30,840
So is g a direct or indirect approach?

1936
01:18:30,840 --> 01:18:32,660
Who thinks direct?

1937
01:18:32,660 --> 01:18:35,500
OK, who thinks indirect?

1938
01:18:35,500 --> 01:18:36,740
Ooh interesting.

1939
01:18:36,740 --> 01:18:37,720
Maybe it's more subtle.

1940
01:18:37,720 --> 01:18:37,900
OK.

1941
01:18:37,900 --> 01:18:40,067
I would have called it direct because we're directly

1942
01:18:40,067 --> 01:18:45,180
mapping from a random variable to a sample from a distribution.

1943
01:18:45,180 --> 01:18:47,160
But with d, it becomes a little more confusing.

1944
01:18:47,160 --> 01:18:49,820
So maybe either answer could have an interpretation

1945
01:18:49,820 --> 01:18:50,940
which is correct.

1946
01:18:50,940 --> 01:18:54,900
So g tries to synthesize synthetic images that

1947
01:18:54,900 --> 01:18:58,100
fool d and--

1948
01:18:58,100 --> 01:18:59,700
that's a typo. g--

1949
01:18:59,700 --> 01:19:01,320
sorry, that should be d.

1950
01:19:05,140 --> 01:19:07,420
d tries to identify the fakes

1951
01:19:07,420 --> 01:19:08,380
OK.

1952
01:19:08,380 --> 01:19:10,080
So why should that work?

1953
01:19:10,080 --> 01:19:13,987
That should work because if g is able to make output data

1954
01:19:13,987 --> 01:19:15,820
points which are indistinguishable from your

1955
01:19:15,820 --> 01:19:18,260
training data points, then it must

1956
01:19:18,260 --> 01:19:21,670
be that you're outputting things that

1957
01:19:21,670 --> 01:19:23,870
are from the same distribution.

1958
01:19:23,870 --> 01:19:25,510
You can prove that, in fact.

1959
01:19:25,510 --> 01:19:28,770
So d plays this game. d is a classifier for,

1960
01:19:28,770 --> 01:19:32,910
Is it a sample from g, my synthetic data,

1961
01:19:32,910 --> 01:19:34,970
or is it a sample from the real world,

1962
01:19:34,970 --> 01:19:37,070
a real photo of flamingos?

1963
01:19:37,070 --> 01:19:39,890
This is just a binary softmax classifier.

1964
01:19:39,890 --> 01:19:44,830
I'm going to try to maximize the probability I place on real

1965
01:19:44,830 --> 01:19:47,010
and minimize the probability I place on fake,

1966
01:19:47,010 --> 01:19:51,710
and that will be a classifier of real versus fake.

1967
01:19:51,710 --> 01:20:01,270
And g tries to fool d by trying to minimize the accuracy that d

1968
01:20:01,270 --> 01:20:04,110
will get on fake, synthetic images.

1969
01:20:04,110 --> 01:20:09,070
So g just minimizes the exact same objective

1970
01:20:09,070 --> 01:20:10,670
that d was maximizing.

1971
01:20:10,670 --> 01:20:16,430
So d is going to try to place, basically, low probability

1972
01:20:16,430 --> 01:20:18,350
on this being a real image.

1973
01:20:18,350 --> 01:20:21,598
And g will simply try to place-- cause

1974
01:20:21,598 --> 01:20:23,890
d to place high probability on this being a real image.

1975
01:20:23,890 --> 01:20:26,150
So it'll try to update its parameters

1976
01:20:26,150 --> 01:20:29,683
to make an image that causes d to get a low score on that,

1977
01:20:29,683 --> 01:20:33,070
on that synthetic photo.

1978
01:20:33,070 --> 01:20:36,130
So this is a min-max game.

1979
01:20:36,130 --> 01:20:37,370
It's an adversarial game.

1980
01:20:37,370 --> 01:20:39,950
But think of it more like a student and a teacher.

1981
01:20:39,950 --> 01:20:42,250
d is the teacher, and g is the student.

1982
01:20:42,250 --> 01:20:43,750
The teacher is saying, hey, you need

1983
01:20:43,750 --> 01:20:45,190
to make a realistic painting, and you

1984
01:20:45,190 --> 01:20:46,130
didn't get it quite right.

1985
01:20:46,130 --> 01:20:47,090
The shadows are wrong.

1986
01:20:47,090 --> 01:20:49,810
So g will update its parameters to make realistic shadows.

1987
01:20:49,810 --> 01:20:51,268
And then d will say, oh, that looks

1988
01:20:51,268 --> 01:20:54,330
indistinguishable from what I consider realistic art,

1989
01:20:54,330 --> 01:20:56,910
so therefore, you've done well.

1990
01:20:56,910 --> 01:21:00,450
So that's a GAN.

1991
01:21:00,450 --> 01:21:02,690
I think-- this is just a summary figure of it.

1992
01:21:02,690 --> 01:21:03,770
I should stop there.

1993
01:21:03,770 --> 01:21:06,353
And we'll continue with two more lectures on generative models

1994
01:21:06,353 --> 01:21:08,180
next week.
